(window.MAP_DATA = window.MAP_DATA || {})["reading_7"] = {"grade":7,"subject":"reading","units":[{"id":"R5","n":5,"title":"RIT 5","goal":"RIT","band":"191–200","skills":["Explains character motivation","Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots in the 6-8 grade band","Uses context to determine word meaning","Makes inferences from literary text"],"vocab":[],"sets":[{"p":0,"goal":"LT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"Why does Hassan resent Yusuf at the beginning of the story?","o":["Yusuf has taken a seat that Hassan was saving for his friend.","Yusuf has told the teacher that Hassan was being unkind.","Yusuf has laughed at Hassan’s jokes in front of the whole class.","Yusuf has refused to work with Hassan on the project."],"k":"A","sk":"Explains character motivation","b":"191–200","g":"LT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"What does Hassan assume about Yusuf’s silence?","o":["that it shows Yusuf is ill","that it shows Yusuf is shy","that it shows Yusuf is proud","that it shows Yusuf is sad"],"k":"C","sk":"Makes inferences from literary text","b":"191–200","g":"LT"}],"set":"1"},{"p":1,"goal":"LT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"Why does Saad laugh at first, even though he does not shout anything?","o":["He tells himself that it is only a harmless joke.","He thinks Majed is laughing at the joke too.","He believes that Majed deserves to be teased by the others.","He wants Ali to choose him as a best friend."],"k":"A","sk":"Explains character motivation","b":"191–200","g":"LT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"What do Majed’s shaking hands in the third paragraph suggest?","o":["He is angry and planning to fight Ali.","He is more hurt by the teasing than he shows.","He is cold because he is sitting outside in the wind.","He is nervous about an exam that day."],"k":"B","sk":"Determines details that reveal characters’ feelings","b":"191–200","g":"LT"}],"set":"2"},{"p":2,"goal":"IT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"What is the central idea of the text?","o":["Coffee drinking began in Yemen and spread across the region and then around the world.","Scholars in Yemen roasted coffee beans so that they could sell them to merchants.","Ethiopia grows more coffee today than any other country in the world.","Coffee was banned in many cities because rulers believed it was unhealthy for people."],"k":"A","sk":"Determines main/central idea in informational text","b":"191–200","g":"IT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"How is the text mainly organized?","o":["as a problem followed by several possible solutions","chronologically, following coffee from its beginning to the present","as a list of the steps for preparing coffee in the traditional Arabian way","as a comparison of coffee and tea in many different countries"],"k":"B","sk":"Identifies chronology as a structure","b":"191–200","g":"IT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"According to the text, why did scholars and worshippers in Yemen drink coffee?","o":["It was cheaper than tea and easier to carry on long journeys.","It was the only drink that could be made in the mountains.","It was given to them as a gift by merchants from Cairo.","It helped them stay awake for long nights of study and prayer."],"k":"D","sk":"Locates details in informational text","b":"191–200","g":"IT"}],"set":"3"},{"p":3,"goal":"IT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"What problem is the Great Green Wall meant to address?","o":["the floods caused by heavy rain near the Red Sea","the shortage of wood for building houses in Africa","the lack of roads across the Sahara Desert","the spread of desert into damaged land in the Sahel"],"k":"D","sk":"Identifies problem-solution as a structure","b":"191–200","g":"IT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"According to the text, which THREE causes have damaged the land?","o":["roads, factories and new cities","droughts, overgrazing and cutting trees","conflict, late funding and slow progress","floods, storms and earthquakes"],"k":"B","sk":"Determines the cause of a situation","b":"191–200","g":"IT"}],"set":"4"},{"p":4,"goal":"VO","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"The root **port** means “carry.” Goods that a country **exports** are goods that it —","o":["sends out to sell in other countries","buys from companies in other countries","keeps in warehouses inside the country","makes in factories for its own people"],"k":"A","sk":"Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots in the 6-8 grade band","b":"191–200","g":"VO"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"Read the sentence. “The weather report was **inaccurate**; it promised sunshine, but it rained all day.” What does **inaccurate** mean?","o":["too long","not correct","very early","very helpful"],"k":"B","sk":"Uses context to determine word meaning","b":"191–200","g":"VO"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"“When the coach announced the team, Salem was **on cloud nine**.” How did Salem feel?","o":["quite tired","very confused","slightly worried","extremely happy"],"k":"D","sk":"Interprets idiom in context","b":"191–200","g":"VO"}],"set":"5"},{"p":null,"goal":"VO","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"The root **aud** means “hear.” An **audible** sound is one that —","o":["can be heard","cannot be stopped","is very loud","is recorded"],"k":"A","sk":"Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots in the 6-8 grade band","b":"191–200","g":"VO"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"Read the sentence. “The ancient manuscript was so **fragile** that the librarian wore gloves and turned each page slowly.” What does **fragile** mean?","o":["very valuable","easily damaged","very heavy","difficult to read"],"k":"B","sk":"Uses context to determine word meaning","b":"191–200","g":"VO"}],"set":"6"},{"p":5,"goal":"LT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"From which point of view is the story told?","o":["first person, by the boy’s uncle","third person, by a narrator who knows only the uncle’s thoughts","third person, by a narrator who knows every passenger’s thoughts","first person, by the boy on the ferry"],"k":"D","sk":"Recognizes first-person point of view","b":"191–200","g":"LT"}],"set":"7"},{"p":6,"goal":"LT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"Who is the speaker of the poem?","o":["a young person walking with his grandfather","an old sailor remembering his voyages to India","the captain of a modern steel ship","a crane standing above the old port"],"k":"A","sk":"Identifies the speaker in poetry","b":"191–200","g":"LT"}],"set":"8"},{"p":9,"goal":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"How does the narrator feel at the end of Text 1?","o":["worried that the eagles will not return in spring","bored and tired from waiting for a long time","annoyed that his cousin woke him so early","amazed and fortunate to have seen the eagles"],"k":"D","sk":"Infers character feelings","b":"191–200","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"}],"set":"9"}]},{"id":"R6","n":6,"title":"RIT 6","goal":"RIT","band":"201–210","skills":["Makes inferences from literary text","Determines the cause of a situation","Determines mood in context","Determines author’s main argument or claim"],"vocab":[],"sets":[{"p":0,"goal":"LT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"Which event is the turning point of the story?","o":["Hassan tells his friends that Yusuf thinks he is better.","Yusuf explains that his father is in hospital in Khartoum.","Mr. Saleh moves Yusuf into the seat beside Hassan.","Yusuf reads the map twice and then smiles at last."],"k":"B","sk":"Identifies the climax or turning point","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"What does the hand-drawn map show about Hassan?","o":["He wants Yusuf to visit the hospital in Khartoum.","He wants Yusuf to find a different seat in class.","He wants to show the class how well he can draw.","He wants to welcome Yusuf and offer him friendship."],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes how a character’s actions reveal change","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"Which sentence BEST states a theme of the story?","o":["New students should try harder to make friends at school.","Group projects are the best way to learn about geography.","People who are quiet usually think they are better than others.","Understanding someone’s situation can turn dislike into kindness."],"k":"D","sk":"Determines theme in literary text","b":"201–210","g":"LT"}],"set":"1"},{"p":1,"goal":"LT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"What does Saad realise in the fourth paragraph?","o":["Ali is not really his friend and has been laughing at him as well.","Majed has been teasing other students when the teachers are not looking.","Laughing along made him part of the cruelty, even without cruel words.","He should tell the head teacher about everything he has seen."],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes how a character’s thoughts develop","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"“His voice was quieter than he wanted, but the boys heard it.” What does this sentence show?","o":["Saad is ill and cannot speak in a loud voice.","The boys are not interested in anything Saad says.","Saad wants to keep his words secret from Majed.","Speaking up is hard for Saad, but he does it anyway."],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes how a character responds to challenges","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"What does Majed’s action of moving his bag off the chair show?","o":["He is making room for Ali and his friends.","He wants Saad to leave him alone in the library.","He accepts Saad’s apology and invites him to sit.","He is getting ready to leave the library."],"k":"C","sk":"Makes inferences from literary text","b":"201–210","g":"LT"}],"set":"2"},{"p":2,"goal":"IT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"How does the section “Across the Region” contribute to the text?","o":["It describes how farmers built terraces to grow coffee on mountainsides.","It shows how the coffee plant first grew wild in the forests of Ethiopia.","It explains how coffee moved to new cities and became part of social life.","It explains why most coffee today is grown in countries far from Arabia."],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes how specific sections contribute to meaning","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"Why were some coffee bans put in place, according to the text?","o":["Some merchants wanted to raise the price of coffee in the markets.","Some farmers wanted people to drink tea grown in their own fields.","Some doctors warned that coffee caused serious illness in young children.","Some rulers worried that people met in coffee houses to criticise them."],"k":"D","sk":"Determines the cause of a situation","b":"201–210","g":"IT"}],"set":"3"},{"p":3,"goal":"IT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"Why did the plan change from a single line of trees?","o":["Experts said such a line would be hard to water and protect.","The trees grew so fast that they blocked the roads.","The countries could not agree on where the line of trees should go.","Farmers refused to allow any trees on their land."],"k":"A","sk":"Understands explicit relationships between ideas","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"What is the central idea of the text?","o":["Farmers in the Sahel have better harvests because rainfall has increased in recent years.","The Great Green Wall has failed completely because there was never enough money to plant and water all of the trees.","The Great Green Wall has changed into a people-centred effort to restore land, with mixed but real results.","The Sahel is a belt of grassland that stretches from the Atlantic Ocean to the Red Sea."],"k":"C","sk":"Determines main/central idea in informational text","b":"201–210","g":"IT"}],"set":"4"},{"p":4,"goal":"VO","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"Which word has the most NEGATIVE connotation?","o":["persistent","committed","stubborn","determined"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes connotative meanings of words","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"Read the paragraph. “Majed was usually **talkative**. In class he answered every question, and at break he told stories until the bell rang. Today, however, he sat alone and said nothing.” Which word in the paragraph signals a contrast?","o":["usually","alone","until","however"],"k":"D","sk":"Identifies words in context that show a contrast","b":"201–210","g":"VO"}],"set":"5"},{"p":null,"goal":"VO","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"Which word suggests the MOST careful way of looking?","o":["glance","glimpse","peek","examine"],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes nuances in meaning among related words","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"In which sentence does **fair** mean “an event with stalls and activities”?","o":["The referee made a fair decision at the end of the match.","She has fair skin and dark eyes.","We visited the science fair at the university.","The weather will be fair tomorrow."],"k":"C","sk":"Determines which meaning of a multiple-meaning word fits","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"The prefix **inter-** means “between.” An **international** match is played —","o":["without any spectators present","between teams from different countries","between two schools in the same city","inside one country only"],"k":"B","sk":"Uses a defined root and/or affix to determine word meaning","b":"201–210","g":"VO"}],"set":"6"},{"p":5,"goal":"LT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"How does the mood change between the second and third paragraphs?","o":["from cheerful and beautiful to tense and uneasy","from tense and uneasy to cheerful and calm again","from bored and sleepy to excited and curious","from sad and quiet to angry and loud"],"k":"A","sk":"Determines mood in context","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"What does the detail that the uncle “was holding the rail too” suggest?","o":["The uncle wanted to show the narrator how to stand safely.","The uncle had never crossed this part of the sea before.","The uncle was also worried, even though he sounded calm.","The uncle was trying to see the gazelles on the islands."],"k":"C","sk":"Makes inferences from literary text","b":"201–210","g":"LT"}],"set":"7"},{"p":6,"goal":"LT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"“Their long necks bent above the quay.” What are the “long necks”?","o":["the ropes of the steel ships","the necks of seabirds","the masts of the old dhows","the arms of the tall cranes"],"k":"D","sk":"Interprets metaphor in context","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"What contrast does the poem develop?","o":["between the speaker’s home in India and his home in Arabia","between the beauty of the steel ships and the ugliness of the dhows","between the busy, lively port of the past and the quiet port of today","between the grandfather’s happiness and the speaker’s sadness"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes how specific parts of a poem contribute to meaning","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"What is the mood of the third stanza’s first two lines?","o":["frightening and dangerous","empty and a little sad","busy and excited","angry and bitter"],"k":"B","sk":"Determines mood in context","b":"201–210","g":"LT"}],"set":"8"},{"p":7,"goal":"IT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"What is the author’s claim?","o":["Families should teach their children to cook healthy meals at home.","Every intermediate school should offer a weekly cooking class.","Fast food is high in salt, sugar and fat and should be banned.","Students should spend less time using their smartphones each day."],"k":"B","sk":"Determines author’s main argument or claim","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"Which reason does the author give in the third paragraph?","o":["Cooking makes students more independent and less reliant on fast food.","Cooking classes are cheaper to run than most other school lessons.","Cooking teaches mathematics, reading and science in a practical way.","Cooking helps families spend more time together in the evenings."],"k":"C","sk":"Identifies reasons that support a claim","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"Which word in the first paragraph creates a contrast to grab the reader’s attention?","o":["yet","simple","every","most"],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes how word choice advances purpose","b":"201–210","g":"IT"}],"set":"9"},{"p":8,"goal":"IT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"What is the author’s claim?","o":["The city should build protected bicycle lanes in every neighbourhood.","Cars should be banned from all streets in the neighbourhood.","Young people should exercise for at least one hour every day.","Parents should allow their children to ride bicycles to school every day."],"k":"A","sk":"Determines author’s main argument or claim","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"Which evidence supports the first reason?","o":["Few people will ride a bicycle when it is 45 °C outside.","Trees planted along the lanes would make the city greener.","A three-kilometre trip takes only about fifteen minutes by bicycle.","Regular exercise strengthens the heart and helps people manage their stress."],"k":"C","sk":"Evaluates evidence used to support claims","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"What counterclaim does the author address?","o":["that children are too young to ride alone","that bicycle lanes are too expensive to build","that cycling is more dangerous than driving","that the summer is too hot for cycling"],"k":"D","sk":"Identifies a counterclaim","b":"201–210","g":"IT"}],"set":"10"},{"p":9,"goal":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"How is the purpose of Text 1 different from the purpose of Text 2?","o":["Text 1 shares a personal experience; Text 2 explains facts about migration.","Both texts persuade readers to visit the hills to watch the birds.","Text 1 persuades readers to protect eagles; Text 2 entertains readers.","Text 1 explains facts about migration; Text 2 shares a personal experience."],"k":"A","sk":"Compares and contrasts multiple texts","b":"201–210","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"What does Badr mean when he says the eagles are “riding the warm air”?","o":["They are flying very fast because the air is hot in the morning.","They are soaring on rising columns of warm air without flapping.","They are flying close to the hot rocks to keep their bodies warm.","They are following the wind that blows from the sea to the land."],"k":"B","sk":"Determines the meaning of a nonliteral phrase","b":"201–210","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"Which idea is shared by BOTH texts?","o":["Bird populations are growing quickly in every part of the world.","Eagles are the only large birds that cross the Arabian Peninsula on their way south.","The migration of large birds over Arabia is an impressive natural event.","People should not watch birds because it disturbs their journey."],"k":"C","sk":"Determines a shared idea in multiple texts","b":"201–210","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"}],"set":"11"},{"p":10,"goal":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"According to Text 2, why did villages often receive electricity later than cities?","o":["Villages were not allowed to buy electricity from the national network.","Cities used up all the electricity, so none was left for the villages.","Building lines to remote areas was expensive, and each village had few customers.","Villagers did not want electricity because they preferred their old kerosene lamps."],"k":"C","sk":"Determines the cause of a situation","b":"201–210","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"Why did the old man in Text 1 cover his eyes?","o":["He was protecting his eyes from the bright sunset.","He was moved to tears by the light after a lifetime of lamps.","He was frightened by the sudden noise of the switch and the bright light.","He was angry that the bulb was above the mosque."],"k":"B","sk":"Makes inferences from literary text","b":"201–210","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"What does **rural** mean in Text 2?","o":["located in the mountains only","expensive and very difficult to build","connected to the national network","in the countryside, away from cities"],"k":"D","sk":"Uses context to determine word meaning","b":"201–210","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"},{"n":4,"t":"mc","s":"Which idea is supported by BOTH texts?","o":["Bringing electricity to villages changed people’s lives in important ways.","Kerosene lamps were safer than electric light bulbs.","Most villages refused to pay for electricity.","Electricity reached small villages long before it reached the large cities nearby."],"k":"A","sk":"Determines a shared idea in multiple texts","b":"201–210","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"}],"set":"12"}]},{"id":"R7","n":7,"title":"RIT 7","goal":"RIT","band":"211–220","skills":["Writes or identifies an objective summary","Identifies a counterclaim and rebuttal","Synthesizes information found in multiple texts","Analyzes the development of theme in literary text"],"vocab":[],"sets":[{"p":0,"goal":"LT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"Which is the BEST objective summary of the story?","o":["Yusuf moves from Sudan, knows a great deal about the Nile and draws an excellent map for a project about African rivers.","Hassan is an unkind boy who makes fun of a new student because of his accent, and the class teacher should have punished him severely for it.","Hassan dislikes Yusuf until a project reveals that Yusuf is worried about his father, and Hassan then reaches out to him in friendship.","Mr. Saleh gives the class a project on rivers, and Hassan and Yusuf get the best mark because Yusuf knows the Nile well."],"k":"C","sk":"Writes or identifies an objective summary","b":"211–220","g":"LT"}],"set":"1"},{"p":1,"goal":"LT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"Which theme is developed through Saad’s experience?","o":["Popular students always decide who is laughed at in class.","Libraries are the safest places in a school to spend break.","Glasses should be repaired properly instead of being taped.","Staying silent while others are hurt is not truly innocent."],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes the development of theme in literary text","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"Which is the BEST objective summary of the story?","o":["Majed’s glasses are broken, so he spends his breaks in the library fixing them with tape while other boys laugh at him.","Saad laughs when others tease Majed, feels guilty after seeing Majed upset, speaks up against the teasing and apologises.","Ali and his friends tease students every morning until a teacher finally discovers what they are doing and stops them.","Saad is a weak boy who follows the crowd and should have been punished for laughing at a classmate with broken glasses."],"k":"B","sk":"Writes or identifies an objective summary","b":"211–220","g":"LT"}],"set":"2"},{"p":2,"goal":"IT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"Which sentence would BEST complete a summary of the text?","o":["Although most coffee is now grown elsewhere, Arab traditions of serving it remain important.","Coffee is a delicious drink, and everyone should try a cup of qahwa at least once in their lives.","The port of Mocha was a beautiful city that many travellers liked to visit.","Coffee houses in Istanbul were the best places in the world to hear the news."],"k":"A","sk":"Summarizes informational text","b":"211–220","g":"IT"}],"set":"3"},{"p":3,"goal":"IT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"What does the word **mosaic** suggest in the third paragraph?","o":["a single forest that covers the region","a mixture of many different kinds of land","a wall made of small coloured stones","a picture painted on the side of a village"],"k":"B","sk":"Determines the meaning of a word used figuratively","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"How does the last paragraph contribute to the text?","o":["It explains exactly where the Sahel begins and where it ends.","It gives instructions for building stone walls.","It describes the history of the Sahara Desert.","It weighs the project’s difficulties against its successes."],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes how specific paragraphs contribute to meaning","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"Which statement would the supporters mentioned in the last sentence MOST LIKELY agree with?","o":["Projects succeed when local people take part in protecting the land.","Trees alone are enough to stop the desert from spreading.","Only national governments can solve the problem of desertification.","The project should return to its original single line of trees."],"k":"A","sk":"Determines author’s or others’ viewpoint","b":"211–220","g":"IT"}],"set":"4"},{"p":4,"goal":"VO","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"The root **chron** means “time.” Which word describes events listed in the order in which they happened?","o":["beneficial","chronological","biographical","geographical"],"k":"B","sk":"Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots in the 6-8 grade band","b":"211–220","g":"VO"}],"set":"5"},{"p":null,"goal":"VO","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"“That project was my brother’s **Achilles’ heel**.” Achilles was a Greek hero who could be hurt only in his heel. What does the phrase mean?","o":["a weak point","a hidden talent","a great success","a painful injury"],"k":"A","sk":"Interprets allusion in context","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"Read the paragraph. “The children were **apprehensive** about the trip. They checked the weather three times, asked whether the bus had a driver they knew, and kept asking what time they would return.” What does **apprehensive** mean?","o":["bored and tired","angry and rude","excited and cheerful","worried and uneasy"],"k":"D","sk":"Uses paragraph-level context to determine word meaning","b":"211–220","g":"VO"}],"set":"6"},{"p":5,"goal":"LT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"“A long, slow swell began to lift the ferry and drop it again, lift it and drop it.” What is the effect of the repeated words?","o":["They suggest that the ferry is about to reach the islands.","They imitate the steady, uncomfortable movement of the boat.","They show that the uncle is repeating the same story.","They show that the narrator cannot remember what happened."],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes the effect of repetition and imagery","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"How does the setting affect the narrator?","o":["The calm water makes him decide to visit the Farasan Islands every year.","The crowded ferry makes him angry with the passengers who are laughing.","The beautiful sunset makes him forget all about the long and tiring journey that still lies ahead of him.","The dark, rough sea frightens him, which makes the sight of the island lights more powerful."],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes how setting affects characters","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"What does the narrator mean by “though perhaps it was only me”?","o":["He wonders whether his own relief, not the sea, made the crossing feel calmer.","He thinks that the uncle did not notice the lights of the islands.","He realizes that the storm had been far worse than anyone had said.","He believes that he was the only passenger who stayed out on the deck in the wind."],"k":"A","sk":"Determines the meaning of a nonliteral phrase","b":"211–220","g":"LT"}],"set":"7"},{"p":6,"goal":"LT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"“He says their sails could hide the sun.” What kind of language is this?","o":["onomatopoeia, imitating the sound of the wind in sails","a fact, describing the size of each sail","hyperbole, to show how many ships there were","a simile, comparing sails to clouds"],"k":"C","sk":"Interprets hyperbole in context","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"What do the final two lines suggest?","o":["The grandfather’s memories bring the old port back to life for the speaker.","The speaker and his grandfather plan to sail to India.","The grandfather has the keys to open the old gates of the port at night.","The port will soon be rebuilt for dhows to use again."],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes the effect of figurative language","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"What is the theme of the poem?","o":["Ports should be kept quiet so that people can rest.","Memories passed between generations keep the past alive.","Steel ships are faster and more useful than wooden dhows.","Old cranes should be removed from modern harbours."],"k":"B","sk":"Determines theme in poetry","b":"211–220","g":"LT"}],"set":"8"},{"p":7,"goal":"IT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"How does the author respond to the counterclaim?","o":["by arguing that schools are not busy at all and have plenty of free time for new subjects","by agreeing that cooking should be taught only at home by families","by ignoring the counterclaim and repeating the first reason again","by admitting the timetable is full and suggesting cooking replace part of a related lesson"],"k":"D","sk":"Identifies a counterclaim and rebuttal","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"Which piece of evidence would MOST strengthen the author’s argument?","o":["a story about a famous chef who learned to cook as an adult","a study showing that students who take cooking classes eat healthier meals","a list of the most popular fast-food restaurants in the city and their prices","a photograph of a modern school kitchen with new equipment"],"k":"B","sk":"Evaluates evidence used to support claims","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"What is the purpose of the question at the end of the text?","o":["to ask readers for information the author does not know","to show that the author is unsure about the claim","to make readers agree by suggesting the answer is obvious","to introduce a new topic that will be discussed later"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes persuasive techniques","b":"211–220","g":"IT"}],"set":"9"},{"p":8,"goal":"IT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"How does the author answer the counterclaim?","o":["by ignoring it and moving on to a new reason","by agreeing in part, then pointing to pleasant months and shady trees","by arguing that the summer is not really hot at all for most people who ride","by saying that people should ride only at night"],"k":"B","sk":"Identifies a counterclaim and rebuttal","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the author describe children riding “between parked cars and fast traffic” in the first paragraph?","o":["to describe the beauty of the streets on winter evenings","to explain the rules of the road to young cyclists in the neighbourhood","to make readers feel the danger and accept the need for lanes","to show that parents are too strict with their children"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes how word choice advances purpose","b":"211–220","g":"IT"}],"set":"10"},{"p":9,"goal":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"Which information from Text 2 explains why the eagles in Text 1 appear only after “the sun warmed the rocks”?","o":["The Arabian Peninsula lies on one of the world’s great flyways for migrating birds.","Thermals form only after the land warms up, so soaring birds start flying later in the morning.","Millions of birds fly south from Europe and Asia every autumn to spend the long winter months in Africa.","Scientists count migrating birds every year to check the health of each species."],"k":"B","sk":"Synthesizes information found in multiple texts","b":"211–220","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"Which detail appears in Text 2 but NOT in Text 1?","o":["The eagles circled higher and higher in the sky without moving their wings.","The birds became visible only as the morning grew warmer.","The birds were on their way to spend the winter in Africa.","Many populations of large birds of prey have fallen in recent decades."],"k":"D","sk":"Compares or contrasts details in multiple texts","b":"211–220","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"}],"set":"11"},{"p":10,"goal":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"Which benefit described in Text 2 connects MOST directly to the old man’s reaction in Text 1?","o":["Pumps could lift water from deep wells.","Refrigerators could keep medicine cold.","Governments paid for power lines as a public service.","Children and adults could read and study after dark."],"k":"D","sk":"Synthesizes information found in multiple texts","b":"211–220","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"How is Text 1 DIFFERENT from Text 2?","o":["Text 1 describes one moment through personal memory; Text 2 explains a general process and its effects.","Text 1 is about cities; Text 2 is about villages.","Text 1 argues for rural electricity; Text 2 argues against it.","Text 1 gives statistics about how electricity was built; Text 2 describes the feelings of one villager."],"k":"A","sk":"Compares and contrasts multiple texts","b":"211–220","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"“Nobody spoke.” What does this short sentence in Text 1 emphasize?","o":["that the narrator could not hear anything","that the villagers were angry about the poles","the deep emotion and wonder of the moment","that the switch had failed to work at first"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes how sentence structure contributes to meaning","b":"211–220","g":"Literary and Informational Text (paired)"}],"set":"12"}]},{"id":"R8","n":8,"title":"RIT 8","goal":"RIT","band":"221–230","skills":["Analyzes inadequately supported claims","Evaluates the effectiveness of an argument"],"vocab":[],"sets":[{"p":8,"goal":"IT","items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"Which sentence is the LEAST supported by evidence?","o":["A trip of three kilometres takes about fifteen minutes by bicycle.","Everybody already agrees that cars are a terrible way to travel.","The weather is pleasant for many months of the year.","Regular exercise strengthens the heart and helps people manage stress."],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes inadequately supported claims","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"How could the author make the last paragraph stronger?","o":["by replacing the exaggerated claim with a reasoned conclusion","by removing the word “Finally” from the first sentence","by repeating the claim from the first paragraph word for word","by adding more information about the hot summer weather"],"k":"A","sk":"Evaluates the effectiveness of an argument","b":"221–230","g":"IT"}],"set":"1"}]}],"mocks":[{"id":"A","note":"Full practice test · 43 questions","sections":[{"passage":{"title":"The Borrowed Book","genre":"story","paras":["Faris had borrowed the library’s only copy of *The Atlas of the Seas* for his geography project. It was a heavy book with a cracked blue cover, and the librarian, Mr. Anwar, had handed it over carefully. “It is very old,” he said. “Please bring it back by Thursday.”","On Wednesday evening, Faris could not find it. He searched his bag, his desk and even the car. His little brother had been playing with his school things, and Faris shouted at him until he cried. Then Faris found the atlas exactly where he had left it, under his own bed, with a corner of the cover torn.","He thought about returning it quietly and saying nothing. The tear was small. Mr. Anwar might not notice.","Instead, on Thursday morning, Faris put the book on the desk and pointed to the tear. “I am sorry,” he said. “I was careless. I can pay for a repair.” Mr. Anwar looked at the corner, then at Faris. “Thank you for telling me,” he said. “Most people would not.” He showed Faris how to mend the cover with special tape, and they fixed it together.","That evening, Faris knocked on his brother’s door. He had one more apology to make."]},"items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"Why does Faris shout at his little brother?","o":["He wrongly believes his brother has lost the atlas.","His brother has torn the cover of the atlas.","His brother has taken the atlas to the library.","He is angry that his brother cannot read the atlas."],"k":"A","sk":"Determines the cause of an event in literary text","b":"191–200","g":"LT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"What choice does Faris face in the third paragraph?","o":["whether to finish his project or not","whether to return the book early or late","whether to hide the damage or admit it","whether to buy a new atlas or borrow one"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes conflict in literary text","b":"191–200","g":"LT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"What does Mr. Anwar’s comment “Most people would not” suggest?","o":["He believes Faris tore the cover on purpose.","He respects Faris for being honest about the damage.","He wants Faris to pay a large amount for the repair.","He thinks most students take good care of books."],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes dialogue to understand characters","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":4,"t":"mc","s":"What does the last paragraph suggest Faris will do?","o":["show his brother how to mend a book cover","tell his brother to stay away from his things","ask his brother to help him with the project","apologize to his brother for shouting at him"],"k":"D","sk":"Makes inferences from literary text","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":5,"t":"mc","s":"Which theme is developed in the story?","o":["Taking responsibility for mistakes earns respect and repairs relationships.","Librarians should check every book carefully when it is returned.","Little brothers often cause trouble when they play with school things.","Old books should never be lent to students because they break so easily."],"k":"A","sk":"Determines theme in literary text","b":"201–210","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"Riyadh Gets a Metro","genre":"informational","paras":["For decades, getting around Riyadh meant one thing: driving. As the city grew to more than seven million people, its roads became some of the busiest in the region. To ease traffic, the city built a metro system, one of the largest projects of its kind in the world.","**A Network Across the City**","The Riyadh Metro opened in stages beginning in late 2024. It has six lines, colour-coded so that passengers can follow them easily, and about 85 stations spread over roughly 176 kilometres. Many sections run underground or on raised tracks above the streets, and the trains are driverless.","**Designed for the Climate**","Because summer temperatures in Riyadh often pass 45 °C, the stations were designed to stay cool. Many have shaded entrances, and some use special glass and building materials that reduce heat from the sun.","**Changing Habits**","Planners hope the metro, together with a large network of buses, will encourage people to leave their cars at home. Fewer cars on the road could mean less traffic, cleaner air and shorter journeys. Whether residents change their habits will become clear over the coming years."]},"items":[{"n":6,"t":"mc","s":"What is the central idea of the text?","o":["Riyadh’s summer temperatures often pass 45 °C, so most people there prefer to drive.","Riyadh built a large metro system to reduce traffic and change how people travel.","The Riyadh Metro is the largest underground railway anywhere in the world.","Riyadh has more than seven million people who all drive to work each day."],"k":"B","sk":"Determines main/central idea in informational text","b":"191–200","g":"IT"},{"n":7,"t":"mc","s":"Why are the metro lines colour-coded?","o":["so that the trains can run without drivers","so that the buses can share the same tracks","so that the stations stay cool in summer","so that passengers can follow them easily"],"k":"D","sk":"Locates details in informational text","b":"191–200","g":"IT"},{"n":8,"t":"mc","s":"What is the main purpose of the section “Designed for the Climate”?","o":["to compare the metro with the city’s network of buses","to explain why the trains in Riyadh do not need drivers","to explain how the stations were planned to deal with the heat","to describe the colours and the names of the six metro lines in the city"],"k":"C","sk":"Determines specific purpose of a subsection","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":9,"t":"ms","s":"According to the text, which TWO benefits could fewer cars bring? Choose TWO answers.","o":["cooler summers","cheaper petrol","cleaner air","more parking fees","shorter journeys"],"k":"C, E","sk":"Determines details that support a concept","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":10,"t":"mc","s":"What does the final sentence suggest about the author’s view?","o":["The author thinks buses are more useful than the new metro trains.","The author believes the metro was a mistake and a waste of money.","The author is certain that most people will stop driving cars very soon.","The author is hopeful but unsure whether the metro will change habits."],"k":"D","sk":"Determines author’s attitude in informational text","b":"211–220","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"The Two Gardeners","genre":"folk tale","paras":["A wealthy merchant wanted the finest garden in the city, so he hired two gardeners and gave each of them half of his land. “In one year,” he said, “I will choose the better garden, and its gardener will stay in my service.”","The first gardener bought fully grown flowering bushes from the market and planted them at once. Within a week his half of the garden was bright with colour, and visitors praised it loudly.","The second gardener spent the first months digging channels, mixing the soil with manure and planting young date palms and small fruit trees. For a long time his half looked like nothing but brown earth and thin green sticks. The first gardener laughed at him.","In the heat of summer, the bought bushes, whose roots had never grown deep, began to dry and die. The young palms and trees, drinking from the channels, grew stronger every week.","At the end of the year, the merchant walked through both halves. Then he turned to the second gardener. “A garden,” he said, “is not judged on the day it is planted, but on the day it is tested.”"]},"items":[{"n":11,"t":"mc","s":"How are the two gardeners’ methods DIFFERENT?","o":["The first digs water channels; the second plants on the dry earth.","The first works alone; the second asks the merchant to help him.","The first wants quick results; the second prepares slowly for the long term.","The first plants young trees; the second buys flowering bushes at the market."],"k":"C","sk":"Compares or contrasts characters","b":"191–200","g":"LT"},{"n":12,"t":"mc","s":"Why do the flowering bushes die in the summer?","o":["The second gardener secretly takes the water from their channels at night.","Their roots have never grown deep enough to survive the heat.","Visitors damage them while walking through the garden.","The merchant forgets to pay for them to be watered."],"k":"B","sk":"Determines the cause of an event in literary text","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":13,"t":"mc","s":"What does the first gardener’s laughter show about him?","o":["He is overconfident and judges only by appearances.","He is surprised by how quickly the trees are growing.","He is kind and wants to cheer up the other gardener.","He is worried that he will lose his job at the end."],"k":"A","sk":"Makes inferences from literary text","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":14,"t":"mc","s":"What does the merchant mean by his final statement?","o":["The second gardener should have planted flowers as well as trees.","He will give both gardeners another year to improve their work.","True quality is shown by how well something lasts under pressure.","Gardens should be planted only in the cool season of the year."],"k":"C","sk":"Determines the meaning of a nonliteral phrase","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":15,"t":"mc","s":"Which proverb BEST matches the lesson of the tale?","o":["Slow and steady preparation brings lasting success.","Many hands make light work, even in a very large garden.","Two heads are always better than one.","The early bird always catches the worm."],"k":"A","sk":"Determines the moral of a folk tale","b":"211–220","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"Desert Rain","genre":"poem","paras":["For months the sky was empty blue,","The ground was cracked, the wells ran low;","The acacia held its breath and knew","That something waited, far and slow.","","Then clouds rolled in like heavy drums,","The first drops hissed on burning stone;","And every creature, when it comes,","Steps out to claim the rain as its own.","","By morning, green has crossed the sand","Like whispers running through a crowd;","The desert, waking, lifts its hand","And thanks the passing, silver cloud."],"poem":true},"items":[{"n":16,"t":"mc","s":"What is the mood of the first stanza?","o":["joyful and loud","sleepy and peaceful","angry and violent","waiting and tense"],"k":"D","sk":"Determines mood in context","b":"191–200","g":"LT"},{"n":17,"t":"mc","s":"“The acacia held its breath.” What does this personification suggest?","o":["The tree has stopped growing completely.","The tree seems to be waiting anxiously for rain.","The wind has stopped blowing through the tree.","The tree is too dry to produce any leaves."],"k":"B","sk":"Interprets personification in context","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":18,"t":"mc","s":"“Then clouds rolled in like heavy drums.” What does this simile emphasize?","o":["the shape of the clouds in the evening sky","the loud music played in the village to celebrate the rain","the dark, powerful arrival of the storm clouds","the slow, quiet movement of the wind"],"k":"C","sk":"Interprets simile in context","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":19,"t":"mc","s":"How does the poem’s structure reflect a change?","o":["It moves from drought, to the storm, to the desert coming back to life.","It moves from happiness, to sadness, to anger at the weather.","It moves from the speaker’s home, to the city, to the open sea.","It moves from early morning, to evening, to the middle of a dark night."],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes how the organization of a poem affects its meaning","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":20,"t":"mc","s":"What does the final image of the desert lifting “its hand” to thank the cloud suggest?","o":["The rain has stopped and the desert is waving goodbye.","The plants are growing so tall that they reach the sky.","The desert is asking the cloud to bring more storms in the coming days.","The land seems grateful for the life the rain has brought."],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes the effect of figurative language","b":"211–220","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"After-School Clubs: Sign-Up Results","genre":"chart","paras":["**Grade 7 club sign-ups (60 students; each student chose one club)**","Football: 21 · Robotics: 15 · Debate: 9 · Art: 8 · Chess: 7","Note: Each club needs at least 8 members to run. The robotics club has only 12 kits, so students may have to share."]},"items":[{"n":21,"t":"mc","s":"Which club was chosen by the most students?","o":["art","football","debate","robotics"],"k":"B","sk":"Interprets information in charts or graphs","b":"191–200","g":"IT"},{"n":22,"t":"mc","s":"What fraction of the students chose robotics?","o":["one third","one quarter","one half","one fifth"],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes quantitative information in informational text","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":23,"t":"mc","s":"Which club will NOT be able to run unless more students join?","o":["art","robotics","debate","chess"],"k":"D","sk":"Integrates information from graphics and text","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":24,"t":"mc","s":"How many robotics students will NOT have a kit of their own?","o":["3","12","15","27"],"k":"A","sk":"Integrates information from graphics and text","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":25,"t":"mc","s":"Which conclusion is BEST supported by the information?","o":["Every club has more members than it needs to run.","Most students prefer quiet indoor clubs to active outdoor clubs.","More than half of the students chose football or robotics.","The chess club is the most popular club in Grade 7."],"k":"C","sk":"Evaluates evidence used to support claims","b":"211–220","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"Why Do We Yawn?","genre":"informational","paras":["Everybody yawns, and so do many animals, from dogs to fish. Yet scientists still do not fully agree on why yawning happens.","For many years, people believed that we yawn because our bodies need more oxygen. However, experiments have not supported this idea. When volunteers breathed air with extra oxygen, they did not yawn less, and when they breathed air with less oxygen, they did not yawn more.","A newer explanation is that yawning helps to cool the brain. A deep yawn draws in a large amount of air and stretches the jaw, which increases blood flow to the head. Some studies have found that people yawn less often when the air around them is very hot, which fits this idea, because hot air could not cool the brain as well.","Yawning is also famously contagious: seeing, hearing or even reading about a yawn can make a person yawn. Some researchers think this may be linked to empathy, our ability to share other people’s feelings, because people often yawn more after seeing family members and friends yawn than after seeing strangers yawn. Have you yawned yet?"]},"items":[{"n":26,"t":"mc","s":"What idea do the experiments in the second paragraph fail to support?","o":["that yawning helps to cool down the brain on hot days","that we yawn because our bodies need more oxygen","that yawning is linked to sharing feelings","that animals yawn for different reasons"],"k":"B","sk":"Identifies evidence that supports a statement","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":27,"t":"mc","s":"According to the text, how might a yawn cool the brain?","o":["It makes the jaw muscles relax and stop working.","It lowers the amount of oxygen that reaches the body.","It stops blood from reaching the head for a few seconds at a time.","It draws in a lot of air and increases blood flow to the head."],"k":"D","sk":"Understands explicit relationships between ideas","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":28,"t":"mc","s":"What does **contagious** mean in the last paragraph?","o":["spreading easily from one person to another","happening only when a person is very tired","causing illness that lasts for a long time","happening at the same time every day"],"k":"A","sk":"Uses context to determine word meaning","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":29,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the author mention that people yawn less in very hot air?","o":["to explain why animals yawn more often than people","to show that the oxygen explanation is correct after all, despite the tests","to give evidence that supports the brain-cooling explanation","to warn readers that hot weather can be dangerous"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes how specific details contribute to meaning","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":30,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the author end with the question “Have you yawned yet?”","o":["to test whether the reader has understood the experiments described earlier","to suggest that the reader should go to sleep early","to introduce a new explanation for why people yawn","to show the reader that reading about yawning can cause a yawn"],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes the purpose of an ending","b":"211–220","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":null,"items":[{"n":31,"t":"mc","s":"The root **spect** means “look.” A **spectator** at a football match is a person who —","o":["plays in the game","watches the game","sells the tickets","trains the players"],"k":"B","sk":"Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots in the 6-8 grade band","b":"191–200","g":"VO"},{"n":32,"t":"mc","s":"Read the sentence. “After the long hike, the boys were **ravenous** and ate everything on the table.” What does **ravenous** mean?","o":["quite angry","very sleepy and tired","extremely hungry","slightly lost"],"k":"C","sk":"Uses context to determine word meaning","b":"191–200","g":"VO"},{"n":33,"t":"mc","s":"In which sentence does **match** mean “a small stick used to start a fire”?","o":["He struck a match to light the camping stove.","The final match will be played on Friday.","No team in the league is a match for ours.","Her scarf is a perfect match for her dress."],"k":"A","sk":"Determines which meaning of a multiple-meaning word fits","b":"191–200","g":"VO"},{"n":34,"t":"mc","s":"“The exam was a piece of cake.” What does this mean?","o":["The exam was given at lunch.","The exam was very short.","The exam was about food.","The exam was very easy."],"k":"D","sk":"Interprets idiom in context","b":"191–200","g":"VO"},{"n":35,"t":"mc","s":"“The old car groaned up the hill.” Which kind of figurative language is used?","o":["hyperbole (exaggeration)","simile","personification","alliteration"],"k":"C","sk":"Interprets personification in context","b":"191–200","g":"VO"},{"n":36,"t":"mc","s":"Which word has the most POSITIVE connotation?","o":["confident","arrogant","conceited","boastful"],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes connotative meanings of words","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":37,"t":"mc","s":"Which word BEST completes the sentence? “The road was blocked by a sandstorm; ___, the bus arrived two hours late.”","o":["similarly","consequently","in contrast","for example"],"k":"B","sk":"Words that show a cause-effect relationship","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":38,"t":"mc","s":"The prefix **mis-** means “wrongly.” What does it mean to **misinterpret** a message?","o":["to send it to the wrong person","to read it again more carefully","to translate it into another language","to understand it in the wrong way"],"k":"D","sk":"Uses a defined root and/or affix to determine word meaning","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":39,"t":"mc","s":"Read the sentence. “Unlike his **cautious** brother, Nawaf jumped into every adventure without thinking.” What does **cautious** mean?","o":["careful to avoid danger","unable to swim well","eager to try new things","quick to lose his temper"],"k":"A","sk":"Uses contrast clues to determine word meaning","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":40,"t":"mc","s":"Which word means almost the same as **reluctant**?","o":["careless","unwilling","excited","surprised"],"k":"B","sk":"Uses synonym relationships","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":41,"t":"mc","s":"“I have told you a thousand times to tidy your room!” This sentence is an example of —","o":["a metaphor","personification","hyperbole","a simile"],"k":"C","sk":"Interprets hyperbole in context","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":42,"t":"mc","s":"The root **dict** means “say.” A **verdict** is —","o":["the decision a judge or jury announces","a book that lists the meanings of words","a speech given at a graduation","a rule that everyone must obey"],"k":"A","sk":"Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots in the 6-8 grade band","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":43,"t":"mc","s":"Read the paragraph. “The village was **isolated**. The nearest town was a day’s drive away, there was no phone signal, and visitors came only twice a year.” What does **isolated** mean?","o":["modern and full of new buildings","far away and cut off from others","crowded and noisy all the time","beautiful and popular with tourists"],"k":"B","sk":"Uses paragraph-level context to determine word meaning","b":"211–220","g":"VO"}]}]},{"id":"B","note":"Full practice test · 43 questions","sections":[{"passage":{"title":"The Lost Ticket","genre":"drama","paras":["*(The waiting hall of a bus station. AMMAR, 13, searches every pocket. His grandfather, ABU AMMAR, sits calmly with a small bag.)*","AMMAR: *(panicking)* It’s gone! The ticket was right here. The bus leaves in ten minutes, and the next one isn’t until tomorrow!","ABU AMMAR: Sit down for one minute.","AMMAR: One minute? We don’t have one minute!","ABU AMMAR: *(gently)* Then we have no time to waste by running in circles. Where did you last see it?","AMMAR: *(stopping, thinking)* At the café… I paid for the tea and put my wallet on the counter… *(His face changes.)* The ticket was inside the menu!","*(AMMAR runs off. ABU AMMAR smiles and looks at his watch. AMMAR returns, out of breath, holding the ticket high.)*","ABU AMMAR: Seven minutes left. You see? Panic is a fast horse that runs in the wrong direction."]},"items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"What problem does Ammar face at the start of the scene?","o":["He cannot find the bus ticket before the bus leaves.","He has lost his grandfather in the busy station.","He has no money left to buy another ticket.","He has missed the bus and must wait until tomorrow."],"k":"A","sk":"Identifies conflict in literary text","b":"191–200","g":"LT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"How are Ammar and his grandfather DIFFERENT in this scene?","o":["Ammar is patient, while his grandfather is in a hurry.","Ammar wants to wait, while his grandfather wants to leave.","Ammar is angry, while his grandfather is frightened.","Ammar panics, while his grandfather stays calm."],"k":"D","sk":"Compares or contrasts characters","b":"191–200","g":"LT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"What do the stage directions *(stopping, thinking)* show about Ammar at that moment?","o":["He is too tired to keep running around the hall.","He is trying to remember the time of the next bus.","He has begun to follow his grandfather’s advice.","He has decided to give up looking for the ticket."],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes use of stage directions","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":4,"t":"mc","s":"What is the theme of the scene?","o":["Grandparents always know where lost things can be found.","Staying calm helps people think clearly and solve problems.","It is dangerous to leave a wallet on a café counter.","Travellers should arrive at the station a day early."],"k":"B","sk":"Determines theme in literary text","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":5,"t":"mc","s":"What does Abu Ammar mean by “Panic is a fast horse that runs in the wrong direction”?","o":["Acting in a panic feels quick but does not lead to solutions.","Running is the best way to deal with a problem quickly.","People in a panic should travel by horse instead of by bus.","A frightened horse can be dangerous to the people near it."],"k":"A","sk":"Interprets metaphor in context","b":"211–220","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"How a Sandstorm Begins","genre":"informational","paras":["A sandstorm can turn a bright afternoon into a brown, choking darkness within minutes. But this dramatic event begins with something simple: wind and loose, dry ground.","When the ground is dry and has few plants to hold it in place, strong winds can lift sand and dust from the surface. Heavier grains of sand bounce along close to the ground, while lighter dust particles can be carried high into the air, sometimes thousands of metres up.","Some of the largest storms form when a mass of cold air moves quickly into an area of hot air. The cold air pushes underneath the warm air and forces it upward, creating strong winds along the boundary. In the Arabian Peninsula, sandstorms are most common in spring and early summer, when the ground is very dry and winds are strong.","Sandstorms can close airports and roads, damage crops and make breathing difficult, especially for people with asthma. Weather services now use satellites to track dust clouds and send warnings so that people can stay indoors, close windows and protect their eyes and mouths."]},"items":[{"n":6,"t":"mc","s":"According to the text, which TWO conditions are needed for a sandstorm to begin?","o":["rain and cold mountain air","heat and very still air","wind and loose, dry ground","clouds and plenty of plants"],"k":"C","sk":"Determines the cause of a situation","b":"191–200","g":"IT"},{"n":7,"t":"mc","s":"How is the movement of heavier sand grains DIFFERENT from that of lighter dust?","o":["Sand moves only at night, while dust moves during the day.","Sand rises very high, while dust stays close to the ground.","Sand is carried far away, while dust falls almost at once.","Sand bounces near the ground, while dust can rise very high."],"k":"D","sk":"Compares or contrasts details/ideas","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":8,"t":"mc","s":"What happens when cold air moves into an area of hot air?","o":["It produces rain that washes the dust out of the air.","It forces the warm air upward and creates strong winds.","It cools the ground and stops the sand from moving.","It pushes the warm air down and makes the wind stop."],"k":"B","sk":"Understands explicit relationships between ideas","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":9,"t":"mc","s":"Which sentence from the text BEST shows how quickly a sandstorm can arrive?","o":["In the Arabian Peninsula, sandstorms are most common in spring and early summer, when the ground is very dry and winds are strong.","Weather services now use satellites to track dust clouds and send warnings so that people can stay indoors, close windows and protect their eyes and mouths.","A sandstorm can turn a bright afternoon into a brown, choking darkness within minutes.","Heavier grains of sand bounce along close to the ground, while lighter dust particles can be carried high into the air, sometimes thousands of metres up."],"k":"C","sk":"Identifies evidence that supports a statement","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":10,"t":"mc","s":"What is the purpose of the last paragraph?","o":["to explain the dangers of sandstorms and how people are warned","to describe how weather satellites are built and then sent into space","to compare sandstorms in Arabia with storms elsewhere","to explain why sandstorms happen mostly in spring"],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes how specific paragraphs contribute to meaning","b":"211–220","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"The Goal That Did Not Count","genre":"story","paras":["With one minute left in the school final, the score was 1–1. Ziyad dribbled past two defenders and shot. The ball hit the post and bounced over the line. The crowd roared. The referee, standing far away, pointed to the centre: goal.","But Ziyad had seen something no one else had. Just before he shot, the ball had touched his hand. It was not deliberate, and it had happened so fast that only he could have noticed.","His teammates lifted him into the air. The opposing goalkeeper, a boy from the neighbouring school, sat on the grass with his head in his hands.","Ziyad’s heart pounded. If he said nothing, his school would win the cup for the first time in ten years. If he spoke, they might lose. He looked at the referee, then at the goalkeeper. Then he walked across the field.","“It touched my hand,” he said.","The referee stared at him for a long moment and then cancelled the goal. The match ended in a draw, and Ziyad’s team lost on penalties. In the changing room, nobody spoke to him. But the next morning, the head teacher read out a letter from the other school, thanking “a young man who showed us what sport is for.”"]},"items":[{"n":11,"t":"mc","s":"What does Ziyad know that the referee does not?","o":["One of his teammates was offside.","The ball touched his hand just before he shot.","The goalkeeper was injured before the shot.","The ball did not cross the goal line."],"k":"B","sk":"Locates details about plot","b":"191–200","g":"LT"},{"n":12,"t":"mc","s":"What is the main conflict Ziyad faces?","o":["He must choose between playing football and studying for exams.","He must decide whether to argue with the referee about a decision.","He must decide whether to take a penalty or let another player.","He must choose between winning the cup and telling the truth."],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes conflict in literary text","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":13,"t":"mc","s":"What does the silence in the changing room suggest?","o":["Ziyad’s teammates are proud of his honest decision.","Ziyad’s teammates are too tired to celebrate the draw.","Ziyad’s teammates are waiting for the head teacher.","Ziyad’s teammates are upset with him for losing the cup."],"k":"D","sk":"Makes inferences from literary text","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":14,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the author describe the goalkeeper sitting “with his head in his hands”?","o":["to show the unfairness of the goal and influence Ziyad’s decision","to show that the goalkeeper has been injured during the last minute of the match","to show that the other school has already given up hope","to show that the goalkeeper is tired after a very long game"],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes how specific details contribute to plot","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":15,"t":"mc","s":"Which theme does the ending develop?","o":["Teams that practise hardest always win in the end.","Honesty can matter more than winning, even when it has a cost.","Referees should use cameras to check every important goal in a final.","Penalty shoot-outs are an unfair way to end a final."],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes the development of theme in literary text","b":"211–220","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"My Brother’s Hands","genre":"poem","paras":["My brother’s hands are cracked and grey","From mixing cement all the day;","They lift the blocks, they shape the wall,","They catch me when I trip and fall.","","He left his books at seventeen","So I could study, sit, and dream;","He never says it, never will,","But every lesson pays his bill.","","So when I write, and when I read,","I know whose hands have met my need;","And one day, when my work is done,","I’ll build a house for him: the one."],"poem":true},"items":[{"n":16,"t":"mc","s":"What does the first stanza show about the brother?","o":["He is angry because he has to work all day.","He wants the speaker to help him build walls.","He does hard physical work but still takes care of the speaker.","He is a student who studies late into the night and sleeps during the day."],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes how specific parts of a poem contribute to meaning","b":"191–200","g":"LT"},{"n":17,"t":"mc","s":"What sacrifice did the brother make?","o":["He left school so that the speaker could continue studying.","He moved to another city to find a better job.","He gave his own house to the speaker’s family to live in.","He stopped playing sports to help his parents."],"k":"A","sk":"Makes inferences from literary text","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":18,"t":"mc","s":"How does the speaker feel about the brother?","o":["mildly annoyed","secretly jealous","deeply grateful","completely confused"],"k":"C","sk":"Determines the speaker’s attitude","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":19,"t":"mc","s":"What does “every lesson pays his bill” mean?","o":["The brother pays the school for every single lesson with his own money.","Each lesson the speaker learns repays the brother’s sacrifice in some way.","Each lesson teaches the speaker how to pay bills correctly.","The speaker earns money by giving lessons to other students."],"k":"B","sk":"Determines the meaning of a nonliteral phrase","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":20,"t":"mc","s":"Why is the promise in the last two lines meaningful?","o":["The speaker wants to become a builder like his brother as soon as he finishes school and leaves home.","The speaker is tired of studying and wants to start working as soon as possible.","The speaker wants to move away from the family and live in his own house.","The speaker plans to use his success to give back to the brother who builds for others."],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes how an ending contributes to meaning","b":"211–220","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"Ibn Sina: The Prince of Physicians","genre":"informational","paras":["Ibn Sina, known in Europe as Avicenna, was born near Bukhara in Central Asia in 980. By the age of eighteen, he had studied logic, mathematics and medicine, and he was already treating patients.","His most famous work, *The Canon of Medicine*, gathered the medical knowledge of the Greek, Persian and Indian traditions and added his own observations. It described diseases, their symptoms and their treatments in an organised way. Ibn Sina recognised that some diseases could spread from person to person, and he recommended testing new medicines carefully before using them widely.","The *Canon* was translated into Latin in the twelfth century and became a standard medical textbook in European universities for hundreds of years. This is one reason Ibn Sina is sometimes called “the Prince of Physicians.”","Ibn Sina was not only a doctor. He wrote on philosophy, astronomy and music, producing a very large number of works during his life. He died in 1037 in Hamadan, in present-day Iran, but his ideas continued to shape medicine long after his death."]},"items":[{"n":21,"t":"mc","s":"What is the central idea of the text?","o":["Medical knowledge came mainly from the Greek tradition in the past.","Ibn Sina was a scholar whose medical work influenced the world for centuries.","European universities were the first to study medicine in an organised way.","Ibn Sina was born near Bukhara and began treating patients at eighteen."],"k":"B","sk":"Determines main/central idea in informational text","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":22,"t":"mc","s":"According to the text, what did *The Canon of Medicine* do?","o":["It explained how to build hospitals in the cities of Central Asia.","It described the life of Ibn Sina and the places he visited.","It argued that Greek medicine was better than Persian medicine.","It organised existing medical knowledge and added new observations."],"k":"D","sk":"Locates details in informational text","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":23,"t":"mc","s":"How is the text mainly organized?","o":["It compares Ibn Sina with several other famous physicians from Europe and Asia.","It presents a problem in medicine and explains how it was solved.","It follows Ibn Sina’s life and work and then explains his lasting influence.","It gives the steps for writing a medical textbook in order."],"k":"C","sk":"Describes the organizational structure of a text","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":24,"t":"mc","s":"Which detail BEST supports the idea that Ibn Sina’s influence lasted a long time?","o":["The Canon was a standard textbook in European universities for centuries.","Ibn Sina died in 1037 in Hamadan, in present-day Iran.","Ibn Sina studied logic, mathematics and medicine as a young man.","Ibn Sina wrote on philosophy, astronomy and music as well as medicine."],"k":"A","sk":"Identifies evidence that supports a statement","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":25,"t":"mc","s":"Which modern medical practice is closest to Ibn Sina’s advice about new medicines?","o":["writing books about the history of medicine","building hospitals near large universities","testing medicines carefully before they are widely used","translating medical books into many different languages"],"k":"C","sk":"Makes inferences from informational text","b":"211–220","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"Water Use in One Home","genre":"chart","paras":["**Average water use per day in the Hamdan family home (litres)**","Showers: 200 · Toilet: 120 · Washing machine: 60 · Kitchen and drinking: 50 · Garden: 170","The family wants to cut its total use by one quarter. A water-saving shower head reduces shower use by half."]},"items":[{"n":26,"t":"mc","s":"What uses the MOST water in the Hamdan home?","o":["the toilet","showers","the garden","the washing machine"],"k":"B","sk":"Interprets information in charts or graphs","b":"191–200","g":"IT"},{"n":27,"t":"mc","s":"What is the family’s total daily water use?","o":["500 litres","550 litres","600 litres","650 litres"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes quantitative information in informational text","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":28,"t":"mc","s":"How many litres a day does the family want to save?","o":["100 litres","150 litres","200 litres","300 litres"],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes quantitative information in informational text","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":29,"t":"mc","s":"Would the water-saving shower head alone allow the family to reach its goal?","o":["No; it saves 100 litres, which is less than the target.","Yes; it saves exactly the amount that the family needs.","No; it does not save any water from the showers at all.","Yes; it saves 200 litres, which is more than the target."],"k":"A","sk":"Integrates information from graphics and text","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":30,"t":"mc","s":"Which change would save the MOST water each day?","o":["halving washing machine use","halving toilet use","halving kitchen and drinking use","halving garden watering"],"k":"D","sk":"Integrates information from graphics and text","b":"211–220","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":null,"items":[{"n":31,"t":"mc","s":"Read the sentence. “The medicine had an **immediate** effect; Saad felt better within minutes.” What does **immediate** mean?","o":["happening at once","quite dangerous","very unusual","happening slowly"],"k":"A","sk":"Uses context to determine word meaning","b":"191–200","g":"VO"},{"n":32,"t":"mc","s":"In which sentence does **bark** mean “the outer covering of a tree”?","o":["The dog’s loud bark woke the whole street early in the morning.","We heard a loud bark from the garden.","Do not bark orders at your classmates.","The bark of the old palm was rough and dry."],"k":"D","sk":"Determines which meaning of a multiple-meaning word fits","b":"191–200","g":"VO"},{"n":33,"t":"mc","s":"“Time flew during the school trip.” What does this mean?","o":["The time seemed to pass very quickly.","The students travelled by aeroplane.","The clocks on the bus were broken.","The trip lasted longer than planned."],"k":"A","sk":"Interprets idiom in context","b":"191–200","g":"VO"},{"n":34,"t":"mc","s":"The suffix **-ist** means “a person who.” A **cyclist** is a person who —","o":["sells tyres","repairs cars","designs roads","rides a bicycle"],"k":"D","sk":"Understands how a suffix changes the meaning of a noun","b":"191–200","g":"VO"},{"n":35,"t":"mc","s":"The root **graph** means “write.” Which word means “the story of a person’s life written by that person”?","o":["photograph","geography","autobiography","paragraph"],"k":"C","sk":"Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots in the 6-8 grade band","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":36,"t":"mc","s":"Which word suggests the STRONGEST feeling of fear?","o":["worried","terrified","uneasy","nervous"],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes nuances in meaning among related words","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":37,"t":"mc","s":"“My grandfather’s memory is a library.” What does this metaphor suggest?","o":["He reads books every single day.","He works in a large public building in the city centre.","He forgets things very easily.","He remembers a great deal of information."],"k":"D","sk":"Interprets metaphor in context","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":38,"t":"mc","s":"Which word BEST completes the sentence? “The first plan was too expensive; ___, the committee chose a cheaper design.”","o":["for instance","therefore","likewise","meanwhile"],"k":"B","sk":"Words that show a cause-effect relationship","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":39,"t":"mc","s":"The root **bene** means “good.” A **beneficial** habit is one that —","o":["has good effects","is hard to stop","is very common","wastes a lot of time"],"k":"A","sk":"Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots in the 6-8 grade band","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":40,"t":"mc","s":"Read the sentence. “Although the instructions seemed **complex**, they were easy once the teacher explained them.” Which word means the OPPOSITE of **complex**?","o":["confusing","difficult","simple","detailed"],"k":"C","sk":"Uses antonym relationships in context","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":41,"t":"mc","s":"Read the paragraph. “The team’s victory was **unprecedented**. In the school’s fifty-year history, no team had ever won the national cup.” What does **unprecedented** mean?","o":["repeated every few years","expected by most people","never having happened before","disappointing to the fans"],"k":"C","sk":"Uses paragraph-level context to determine word meaning","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":42,"t":"mc","s":"Which word has the most NEGATIVE connotation?","o":["scrawny","slender","lean","slim"],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes connotative meanings of words","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":43,"t":"mc","s":"Which analogy is CORRECT? **Pen** is to **writer** as **brush** is to —","o":["driver","painter","teacher","doctor"],"k":"B","sk":"Uses analogies to understand word relationships","b":"211–220","g":"VO"}]}]},{"id":"C","note":"Full practice test · 43 questions · harder","sections":[{"passage":{"title":"The Farasan Islands","genre":"informational","paras":["About forty kilometres off the coast of Jizan, in the southern Red Sea, lie the Farasan Islands, a group of more than eighty islands. Only a few of them are inhabited, and much of the area is a protected nature reserve.","**Wildlife on Land and Sea**","The islands are home to the Farasan gazelle, a small gazelle found nowhere else in the world. Their shallow waters contain coral reefs, sea turtles and dugongs, large sea mammals that graze on seagrass. Every year, thousands of migrating birds rest on the islands on their journeys between Europe, Asia and Africa.","**A Fishing Festival**","Each spring, large shoals of parrotfish arrive in a bay on the main island. For many years, local people have celebrated their arrival with a traditional festival, in which fishermen gather the fish together while families watch and sing.","**Protecting the Islands**","As more visitors discover the islands, the reserve faces new pressures. Rules limit fishing in some areas and protect the beaches where turtles lay their eggs. Scientists hope that careful tourism can bring income to local families without harming the wildlife that makes the islands special."]},"items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"What makes the Farasan gazelle special?","o":["It is found nowhere else in the world.","It migrates between Europe and Africa.","It lives in the sea and eats seagrass.","It is the largest gazelle in Arabia."],"k":"A","sk":"Locates details in informational text","b":"191–200","g":"IT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"What is a dugong, according to the text?","o":["a migrating bird that rests on the beaches","a kind of parrotfish that arrives in spring","a small gazelle that lives on the islands","a large sea mammal that grazes on seagrass"],"k":"D","sk":"Determines domain-specific word meaning in context","b":"191–200","g":"IT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"What is the central idea of the text?","o":["Fishing is the most important source of money for families on the islands.","The Farasan Islands have rare wildlife and traditions that people are working to protect.","Tourists should not be allowed to visit the Farasan Islands at all.","The Farasan Islands are the largest and most crowded group of islands anywhere in the Red Sea."],"k":"B","sk":"Determines main/central idea in informational text","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":4,"t":"mc","s":"What is the purpose of the section “Protecting the Islands”?","o":["to list the birds that rest on the islands each year","to describe the traditional songs that families sing together at the festival","to explain the pressures on the reserve and how they are managed","to explain how coral reefs grow in shallow water"],"k":"C","sk":"Determines specific purpose of a subsection","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":5,"t":"mc","s":"What do scientists hope careful tourism will achieve?","o":["income for local families without harm to the wildlife","larger festivals with visitors from many countries","more fishing in the protected areas of the reserve for local families","new hotels on every one of the eighty islands"],"k":"A","sk":"Determines details that support a stated idea","b":"211–220","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"The Chess Club Election","genre":"story","paras":["Everyone expected Waleed to become captain of the chess club. He had won the school tournament two years in a row, and he never let anyone forget it. When the teacher announced the election, Waleed did not even make a speech. “Everyone knows I’m the best,” he said.","The other candidate was Nabil, a quiet boy who had lost to Waleed in both finals. In his speech, Nabil did not talk about winning. He talked about the younger members who were afraid to play because they kept losing. “I want to start a beginners’ table,” he said, “where nobody laughs at a bad move.”","When the votes were counted, Nabil had won by eleven votes to nine. Waleed stared at the board. “But I’m the best player,” he said, almost to himself.","“You are,” said the teacher. “But the captain isn’t chosen for how he plays. He’s chosen for how he helps others play.”","For a week, Waleed stayed away from the club. Then, one afternoon, Nabil found him standing at the door of the classroom. “The beginners’ table needs someone who can explain openings,” Nabil said. “Nobody explains them better than you.” Waleed hesitated. Then he pulled out a chair."]},"items":[{"n":6,"t":"mc","s":"What does Waleed’s comment “Everyone knows I’m the best” show about him?","o":["He is modest and does not like to speak in public.","He is overconfident and assumes skill alone will win.","He is unsure whether he really wants the role.","He is nervous and hides his worry with jokes."],"k":"B","sk":"Describes character traits; supporting evidence","b":"191–200","g":"LT"},{"n":7,"t":"mc","s":"How is Nabil’s speech DIFFERENT from Waleed’s approach?","o":["Nabil makes no speech, while Waleed gives a long and careful one.","Nabil criticises the teacher, while Waleed praises the club members.","Nabil talks about winning, while Waleed talks about the beginners.","Nabil focuses on helping others, while Waleed relies on his record."],"k":"D","sk":"Compares or contrasts characters","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":8,"t":"mc","s":"What does the teacher mean by her comment about the captain?","o":["Leadership is decided by the teacher, not by the members.","The captain should play only against the strongest members.","Leadership depends on supporting others, not only on personal skill.","The captain must win every tournament that the chess club enters each year."],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes dialogue to understand characters","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":9,"t":"mc","s":"Why does Nabil invite Waleed to the beginners’ table?","o":["He values Waleed’s skill and wants to include him.","He wants Waleed to apologise to the younger members.","He wants to show the club that he defeated Waleed.","He needs Waleed to vote for him in the next election."],"k":"A","sk":"Explains character motivation","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":10,"t":"mc","s":"What does Waleed’s final action suggest about how he has changed?","o":["He still believes he should have won the election.","He has decided to leave the chess club forever.","He is ready to use his talent to help others.","He wants to challenge Nabil to another final."],"k":"C","sk":"Understands how characters are developed or changed","b":"211–220","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"Night in the Asir Mountains","genre":"poem","paras":["The fog comes climbing up the slope","On quiet feet, like grey wool spun;","It wraps the juniper and rope","And buries every hint of sun.","","Below, the valley lights go out","Like candles pinched by careful hands;","Above, the stars begin to shout","Across the high and silent lands.","","I pull my blanket to my chin","And listen to the mountain breathe;","The cold stays out, the stars stay in,","And dreams come up from far beneath."],"poem":true},"items":[{"n":11,"t":"mc","s":"“The fog comes climbing up the slope / On quiet feet.” What does this personification suggest?","o":["The fog is loud and moves very fast.","People are climbing the steep mountain slowly in the thick fog.","The fog is frightening and dangerous.","The fog moves slowly and silently up the mountain."],"k":"D","sk":"Interprets personification in context","b":"191–200","g":"LT"},{"n":12,"t":"mc","s":"“Like candles pinched by careful hands.” What does this simile describe?","o":["people lighting candles in the valley","the valley lights going out one by one","the fog covering the juniper trees","the stars appearing in the night sky"],"k":"B","sk":"Interprets simile in context","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":13,"t":"mc","s":"What is the overall mood of the poem?","o":["busy and noisy","tense and frightening","sad and lonely","calm and full of wonder"],"k":"D","sk":"Determines mood in context","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":14,"t":"mc","s":"Why might the poet say the stars “begin to shout”?","o":["to show that the speaker is afraid of the dark","to show how bright and clear the stars look","to show that the stars make a loud noise","to show that a storm is coming over the mountains"],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes the effect of figurative language","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":15,"t":"mc","s":"How does the focus of the poem change in the last stanza?","o":["It moves from calm to fear as the storm arrives.","It moves from night to the bright light of morning.","It moves from the landscape to the speaker’s own peaceful feelings.","It moves from the speaker’s home to a journey across the valley."],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes how specific parts of a poem contribute to meaning","b":"211–220","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"The Poet and the Caliph’s Riddle","genre":"folk tale","paras":["Long ago, a caliph who loved riddles announced a contest. “I will give a bag of gold,” he declared, “to anyone who can bring me something that is heavier when it is empty than when it is full.”","Merchants arrived with clever boxes and strange jars, but none of them could answer. Scholars argued for days. At last, a poor young poet asked to speak.","“Commander of the Faithful,” he said, “a purse is heavy on the heart when it is empty, and light when it is full.” The court laughed, and even the caliph smiled.","“A good answer,” said the caliph, “but not the one I meant.” Then he turned to his guards. “Give the young man the bag of gold anyway.”","The court was astonished. “Why reward a wrong answer?” asked the chief minister.","“Because,” said the caliph, “the merchants brought me things, and the scholars brought me arguments. He alone brought me the truth about his own life. A ruler should listen to the empty purse as carefully as to the full one.”"]},"items":[{"n":16,"t":"mc","s":"What is unusual about the riddle the caliph sets?","o":["It asks for something heavier when empty than when full.","It asks for something that no merchant can sell.","It must be answered in the form of a poem.","It can be answered only by people who own a great deal of gold."],"k":"A","sk":"Locates details about plot","b":"191–200","g":"LT"},{"n":17,"t":"mc","s":"How does the poet answer the riddle?","o":["He brings a jar that grows heavier as it empties.","He uses the purse as an image of how poverty feels.","He argues that the riddle has no correct answer.","He asks the scholars to help him find the answer."],"k":"B","sk":"Interprets figurative language in context","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":18,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the caliph reward the poet even though the answer is “not the one I meant”?","o":["The caliph forgot what the correct answer was supposed to be.","The caliph wanted to embarrass the merchants and scholars.","The poet’s answer revealed an honest truth about the poor.","The poet was the only person who tried to answer at all."],"k":"C","sk":"Explains character motivation","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":19,"t":"mc","s":"What does the caliph mean by “listen to the empty purse as carefully as to the full one”?","o":["A ruler should pay attention to poor people as well as rich ones.","A ruler should count all the money in the treasury every single day.","A ruler should give purses of gold to all his visitors.","A ruler should never trust merchants who carry money."],"k":"A","sk":"Determines the meaning of a nonliteral phrase","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":20,"t":"mc","s":"What is the message of the tale?","o":["Poets are cleverer than merchants and scholars.","Gold should only be given for correct answers.","Riddles are a waste of time for busy rulers who have important work to do.","Wisdom and justice require listening to those with little power."],"k":"D","sk":"Determines theme in literary text","b":"211–220","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"How Grade 7 Gets to School","genre":"chart","paras":["**Survey of 80 Grade 7 students: usual way of getting to school**","Family car: 44 · School bus: 20 · Walking: 10 · Bicycle: 4 · Metro or public bus: 2","The school plans to add a second bus route next term. Each bus carries 30 students."]},"items":[{"n":21,"t":"mc","s":"How many students walk or cycle to school?","o":["4","10","14","24"],"k":"C","sk":"Interprets information in charts or graphs","b":"191–200","g":"IT"},{"n":22,"t":"mc","s":"What percentage of the students travel by family car?","o":["44%","45%","50%","55%"],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes quantitative information in informational text","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":23,"t":"mc","s":"How many more students could travel by school bus once the second route is added?","o":["20","30","40","60"],"k":"B","sk":"Integrates information from graphics and text","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":24,"t":"mc","s":"Which claim is BEST supported by the survey?","o":["Most students depend on a family car to get to school.","Walking to school is more popular than the school bus.","Public transport is the fastest way to reach the school.","Most students would prefer to cycle if it were safer."],"k":"A","sk":"Evaluates evidence used to support claims","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":25,"t":"mc","s":"What is the MOST LIKELY reason the school plans a second bus route?","o":["to reduce the number of students who come by car","to help the students who cycle to school every day","to make the walk to school shorter","to replace the metro and public buses"],"k":"A","sk":"Makes inferences from informational text","b":"211–220","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"Why Teenagers Feel Awake at Midnight","genre":"informational","paras":["Many parents complain that their teenage children stay up late and are impossible to wake in the morning. It is tempting to blame phones or laziness, but scientists say biology also plays a part.","Every person has a body clock, called a **circadian rhythm**, that controls when we feel sleepy and when we feel alert. One of its signals is a hormone called **melatonin**, which the body releases in the evening to prepare for sleep. During the teenage years, this release often shifts later, sometimes by two hours or more. As a result, many teenagers simply do not feel sleepy until late at night.","At the same time, experts recommend that teenagers sleep eight to ten hours a night. A student who falls asleep at midnight but must wake at 6 a.m. gets only six hours, and the shortfall builds up during the week. Lack of sleep is linked to poorer concentration, worse mood and slower reaction times.","Phones can make the problem worse, because bright screens late at night can delay sleep even further. Experts therefore suggest putting devices away an hour before bed, keeping a regular bedtime even at weekends, and spending time in daylight in the morning to help reset the body clock."]},"items":[{"n":26,"t":"mc","s":"According to the text, what is one biological reason teenagers stay up late?","o":["Teenagers need much less sleep than younger children and adults do each night.","The circadian rhythm stops working properly during the teenage years.","The evening release of melatonin often shifts later during the teenage years.","Teenagers produce more melatonin in the morning than in the evening."],"k":"C","sk":"Determines the cause of a situation","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":27,"t":"mc","s":"What is a **circadian rhythm**, as described in the text?","o":["a pattern of dreams that repeats each night","a hormone that the body releases in the morning to wake people up","a type of exercise that improves sleep","a body clock that controls when we feel sleepy or alert"],"k":"D","sk":"Determines domain-specific word meaning in context","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":28,"t":"mc","s":"Which piece of advice is NOT given in the text?","o":["spend time in daylight in the morning","sleep for a few hours during the school day","put all devices away an hour before going to bed","keep a regular bedtime even at weekends"],"k":"B","sk":"Locates details in informational text","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":29,"t":"mc","s":"How many hours short of the minimum recommendation is the student who sleeps from midnight to 6 a.m.?","o":["2 hours","4 hours","6 hours","8 hours"],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes quantitative information in informational text","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":30,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the author mention phones only in the last paragraph?","o":["to explain how phones help the body clock reset itself","to suggest that teenagers should not own phones at all","to prove that phones are the main and only cause of teenage tiredness","to show that phones add to a problem that biology already causes"],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes how specific paragraphs contribute to meaning","b":"221–230","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":null,"items":[{"n":31,"t":"mc","s":"The root **port** means “carry.” Which word means “able to be carried easily”?","o":["airport","portable","passport","important"],"k":"B","sk":"Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots in the 6-8 grade band","b":"191–200","g":"VO"},{"n":32,"t":"mc","s":"“When Hamad saw the exam questions, his heart sank.” What does this mean?","o":["He felt a sharp pain in his chest.","He was very pleased with the questions.","He suddenly felt disappointed and worried.","He forgot everything he had studied."],"k":"C","sk":"Interprets idiom in context","b":"191–200","g":"VO"},{"n":33,"t":"mc","s":"The prefix **sub-** means “under.” A **submarine** travels —","o":["under the water","above the clouds","across the desert","through the mountains"],"k":"A","sk":"Uses a defined root and/or affix to determine word meaning","b":"191–200","g":"VO"},{"n":34,"t":"mc","s":"“The wind whispered through the palm trees.” This sentence uses —","o":["an idiom","a simile using “like”","personification","hyperbole"],"k":"C","sk":"Interprets personification in context","b":"191–200","g":"VO"},{"n":35,"t":"mc","s":"Read the sentence. “The singer’s voice was so **melodious** that the audience sat in silence to listen.” What does **melodious** mean?","o":["fast and confusing","loud and harsh","weak and shaky","pleasant and musical"],"k":"D","sk":"Uses context to determine word meaning","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":36,"t":"mc","s":"Which word has the most POSITIVE connotation?","o":["tight-fisted","thrifty","stingy","mean"],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes connotative meanings of words","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":37,"t":"mc","s":"In which sentence does **current** mean “happening now”?","o":["The river’s current was strong after the rain.","Read the current news about the election.","The current pulled the swimmer out to sea.","An electric current flows through the wire."],"k":"B","sk":"Determines which meaning of a multiple-meaning word fits","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":38,"t":"mc","s":"Which word BEST completes the sentence? “The book was long; ___, it was so exciting that I finished it in two days.”","o":["similarly","therefore","nevertheless","for example"],"k":"C","sk":"Identifies words in context that show a contrast","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":39,"t":"mc","s":"Which word means the OPPOSITE of **scarce**?","o":["limited","rare","missing","plentiful"],"k":"D","sk":"Identifies antonyms","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":40,"t":"mc","s":"“Hassan’s new phone was his pride and joy.” What does this phrase mean?","o":["something he was very proud of and loved","something that often made him feel ashamed","something he wanted to sell quickly","something that often stopped working"],"k":"A","sk":"Interprets idiom in context","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":41,"t":"mc","s":"Read the paragraph. “Turki was **meticulous** about his project. He checked every measurement twice, labelled every drawing and rewrote any sentence that was not perfectly clear.” What does **meticulous** mean?","o":["quick, careless and messy","bored and uninterested","very careful about details","proud and boastful"],"k":"C","sk":"Uses paragraph-level context to determine word meaning","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":42,"t":"mc","s":"The root **chron** means “time.” Two clocks that are **synchronized** —","o":["run at different speeds","show exactly the same time","are made by the same company","need new batteries"],"k":"B","sk":"Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots in the 6-8 grade band","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":43,"t":"mc","s":"Which analogy is CORRECT? **Thermometer** is to **temperature** as **clock** is to —","o":["distance","weight","speed","time"],"k":"D","sk":"Uses analogies to understand word relationships","b":"211–220","g":"VO"}]}]},{"id":"D","note":"Full practice test · 43 questions · harder","sections":[{"passage":{"title":"The Twins’ Project","genre":"story","paras":["Anas and Iyad were twins, but nobody who knew them confused them for long. Anas was loud and full of ideas; Iyad was quiet and good with his hands. For the regional science fair, they built a water filter from sand, gravel and charcoal. Anas designed the poster. Iyad spent three weekends testing the filter until the dirty water came out clear.","On the day of the fair, the judges stopped at their table. Anas stepped forward and explained everything: the layers, the tests, the results. He used the word “I” many times. Iyad stood behind the table and said nothing.","When the twins won second prize, a reporter from the local newspaper asked Anas for a photograph. Iyad watched his brother hold the certificate and smile. He felt something tighten in his chest.","That evening, their father asked Iyad how the fair had gone. “Anas explained it very well,” Iyad said. His father looked at him for a while. “And who made the water clear?” he asked. Iyad looked at the floor.","The next morning, Iyad found a copy of the newspaper on the kitchen table. Under the photograph, someone had crossed out “Anas Al-Harbi” in pen and written beside it, in his brother’s untidy handwriting: “Anas and Iyad Al-Harbi. Iyad did the hard part.”"]},"items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"How are the twins DIFFERENT, according to the first paragraph?","o":["Anas is outgoing and full of ideas, while Iyad is quiet and practical.","Anas is quiet and practical, while Iyad is outgoing and full of ideas.","Anas enjoys science, while Iyad prefers art and drawing posters.","Anas works slowly and carefully, while Iyad likes to finish quickly."],"k":"A","sk":"Compares or contrasts characters","b":"191–200","g":"LT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"What does the detail that Anas “used the word ‘I’ many times” suggest?","o":["He was nervous and repeated himself to the judges.","He did not understand how the filter worked.","He wanted the judges to remember his first name.","He took most of the credit for work they had shared."],"k":"D","sk":"Determines details that support an inference","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"“He felt something tighten in his chest.” What does this suggest about Iyad?","o":["He felt ill after standing in the hall all day.","He felt hurt that his work was not recognized.","He felt proud that the project had won a prize.","He felt nervous about speaking to the reporter."],"k":"B","sk":"Determines details that reveal characters’ feelings","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":4,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the father ask, “And who made the water clear?”","o":["to find out whether the filter really worked at the fair","to criticise Iyad for not speaking to the judges","to show Iyad that he knows Iyad’s contribution mattered","to ask Iyad to explain the science of the filter"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes dialogue to understand characters","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":5,"t":"mc","s":"What does Anas’s note on the newspaper reveal?","o":["He is angry that the local newspaper printed the wrong name under the photo.","He wants Iyad to give the next presentation by himself.","He believes the project should have won first prize.","He recognises his mistake and gives his brother public credit."],"k":"D","sk":"Understands how characters are developed or changed","b":"211–220","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"City Bike Rental: Price List","genre":"chart","paras":["**City Bike Rental**","First 30 minutes: free · Each extra 30 minutes (or part of 30 minutes): 5 riyals · Full day (up to 10 hours): 40 riyals","Students with a valid school card pay half price for full-day rentals.","Bikes must be returned to any city station. Late return after 10 p.m.: 20-riyal fine."]},"items":[{"n":6,"t":"mc","s":"What is the purpose of the last line?","o":["to explain how to unlock a bike at a station","to advertise the free first thirty minutes","to warn riders about a penalty for returning bikes late","to tell riders where they can buy a school card for discounts"],"k":"C","sk":"Determines specific purpose of a text feature","b":"191–200","g":"IT"},{"n":7,"t":"mc","s":"How much does a 45-minute ride cost?","o":["5 riyals","20 riyals","nothing","10 riyals"],"k":"A","sk":"Integrates information from a text feature","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":8,"t":"mc","s":"How much does a student with a school card pay for a full-day rental?","o":["10 riyals","20 riyals","40 riyals","5 riyals"],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes quantitative information in informational text","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":9,"t":"mc","s":"How much does a two-hour ride cost?","o":["15 riyals","20 riyals","40 riyals","10 riyals"],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes quantitative information in informational text","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":10,"t":"mc","s":"For which rider would a full-day rental be the BEST choice?","o":["a student riding for twenty minutes","a student riding for forty minutes","an adult riding for exactly one hour","an adult planning to ride for six hours"],"k":"D","sk":"Integrates information from graphics and text","b":"211–220","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"Old Photograph","genre":"poem","paras":["In black and white my grandfather","Stands straight beside a dusty truck;","His smile is young, his eyes are sure,","His hand is raised as if for luck.","","I do not know the road he took,","Or what the truck was carrying then;","I only have this single look","Of who he was before the men","","Who call him “Hajji” at the door,","Who help him gently to his chair.","I hold the picture up once more —","And find him, laughing, waiting there."],"poem":true},"items":[{"n":11,"t":"mc","s":"What does the photograph show?","o":["the grandfather as an old man sitting quietly in his chair at home","the speaker standing beside the grandfather’s truck","the grandfather as a confident young man beside a truck","a group of men visiting the grandfather’s house"],"k":"C","sk":"Locates details in poetry","b":"191–200","g":"LT"},{"n":12,"t":"mc","s":"What contrast does the poem develop?","o":["between the grandfather’s truck and the speaker’s bicycle","between the grandfather’s strong youth and his frail old age","between the speaker’s city life and the grandfather’s village","between black-and-white photographs and colour photographs"],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes how specific parts of a poem contribute to meaning","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":13,"t":"mc","s":"What does the speaker admit in the second stanza?","o":["He has many photographs of his grandfather.","He knows very little about his grandfather’s early life.","He has driven the same long road as his grandfather many times.","He does not like looking at old photographs."],"k":"B","sk":"Determines details that reveal the speaker’s thoughts","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":14,"t":"mc","s":"What do the final lines suggest?","o":["The photograph lets the speaker find the young grandfather again.","The grandfather has become young again after recovering from a long illness.","The speaker has decided to frame the photograph and sell it.","The grandfather is laughing at the men who help him."],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes how an ending contributes to meaning","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":15,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the second stanza run directly into the third without a full stop?","o":["to show that the speaker has forgotten how to end the sentence","to show that the truck is still driving along the road","to connect the young man in the photo with the old man of today","to show that the men at the door are in a hurry"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes how the organization or form of a poem affects its meaning","b":"221–230","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"Al-Idrisi’s Map of the World","genre":"informational","paras":["In the twelfth century, King Roger II of Sicily wanted the most accurate description of the world that anyone could produce. He invited a geographer named Muhammad al-Idrisi, who had been born in Ceuta in North Africa and had studied in Córdoba, to his court in Palermo.","Al-Idrisi did not simply copy older maps. For about fifteen years, he gathered information from earlier geographers, compared their works, and questioned travellers, merchants and sailors who passed through Sicily. When reports disagreed, he tried to keep only the information that several sources confirmed.","In 1154, he completed a large book of geography together with a world map. Unlike most maps today, his map placed south at the top. It showed Europe, Asia and North Africa in remarkable detail, with rivers, mountains and cities, though lands far from the trade routes were much less accurate.","Al-Idrisi’s work was copied and studied for centuries. It remains one of the most important examples of how medieval scholars combined careful research with the experience of ordinary travellers to describe the world."]},"items":[{"n":16,"t":"mc","s":"What is the central idea of the text?","o":["King Roger II of Sicily was the first ruler in history to ask for a complete map of the world.","Medieval maps were less accurate than maps that are made today.","Al-Idrisi was born in Ceuta and later studied in the city of Córdoba.","Al-Idrisi created a detailed world map through careful research and travellers’ reports."],"k":"D","sk":"Determines main/central idea in informational text","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":17,"t":"mc","s":"How did al-Idrisi deal with reports that disagreed?","o":["He chose the report from the most famous traveller.","He asked the king to decide which report was true.","He kept information that several sources confirmed.","He drew every version on the map side by side."],"k":"C","sk":"Locates details in informational text","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":18,"t":"mc","s":"What does the word **remarkable** suggest about the detail of the map?","o":["It was copied from much older maps.","It was limited to the island of Sicily.","It was confusing, crowded and difficult to read.","It was impressive and unusual for its time."],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes how word choice advances purpose","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":19,"t":"mc","s":"Which detail explains why some distant lands on the map were less accurate?","o":["They were far from the trade routes used by his sources.","He placed south at the top of the map.","He completed the map in the year 1154.","He worked on the map for about fifteen years in Palermo."],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes implicit relationships between ideas","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":20,"t":"mc","s":"What is the purpose of the final paragraph?","o":["to explain how the map was copied by hand","to explain why al-Idrisi’s work is still considered important","to compare al-Idrisi’s map with modern maps","to describe the many travellers and sailors who visited Palermo"],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes how specific paragraphs contribute to meaning","b":"211–220","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"The Referee","genre":"drama","paras":["*(The edge of a school football pitch at half-time. TARIQ, 14, is a student referee. COACH HAMDI, from the home team, walks up to him quickly.)*","COACH HAMDI: That penalty in the first half was a joke. My player hardly touched him.","TARIQ: *(holding his whistle tightly)* I was standing two metres away, Coach. Your player pushed him from behind.","COACH HAMDI: *(lowering his voice)* Listen. You’re a good student. The head of sports is my friend. A referee who wants to referee the final next month… needs to see the game the right way.","TARIQ: *(after a pause)* I see the game from two metres away. That’s the right way.","COACH HAMDI: *(staring at him, then walking off)* We’ll see.","*(A teacher, MR. FAHAD, who has been standing nearby, steps forward.)*","MR. FAHAD: I heard all of that. *(He smiles.)* I think I know who should referee the final."]},"items":[{"n":21,"t":"mc","s":"What is the conflict in the scene?","o":["A teacher refuses to let the student referee continue the match.","Two teams argue about who should play in next month’s final.","A student referee cannot decide whether a penalty should be given.","A coach pressures a student referee to change how he judges the game."],"k":"D","sk":"Identifies conflict in literary text","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":22,"t":"mc","s":"What does the stage direction *(holding his whistle tightly)* suggest?","o":["Tariq is afraid of losing his whistle in the crowd.","Tariq feels tense but is determined to stand firm.","Tariq is bored and waiting for the second half.","Tariq is about to blow the whistle to end the match."],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes use of stage directions","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":23,"t":"mc","s":"What is the coach really suggesting when he mentions the final?","o":["Tariq should rest before refereeing the final next month.","Tariq is not good enough to referee an important match.","Tariq will be rewarded if he favours the coach’s team.","Tariq should ask the head of sports for advice."],"k":"C","sk":"Makes inferences from literary text","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":24,"t":"mc","s":"What does Tariq mean by “I see the game from two metres away. That’s the right way.”?","o":["He will judge only by what he honestly saw.","He prefers to stand far away from the players.","He agrees to change his decisions in the second half.","He cannot see the game clearly from where he stands."],"k":"A","sk":"Determines the meaning of a nonliteral phrase","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":25,"t":"mc","s":"How does Mr. Fahad’s final line resolve the tension?","o":["It suggests the penalty decision will be changed after all.","It suggests Tariq will be removed from refereeing duties.","It suggests the coach will become the referee for the final.","It suggests Tariq’s honesty will be rewarded, not punished."],"k":"D","sk":"Identifies events that lead to resolution","b":"211–220","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"Every Student Should Learn First Aid","genre":"argument","paras":["Imagine a classmate collapses in the playground. The teacher is on the other side of the school. Would you know what to do? For most students, the honest answer is no. Basic first aid should be a required part of the intermediate curriculum.","First, emergencies happen everywhere, not only in hospitals. Choking, burns and serious falls can happen at home, at school or on a family trip. In the minutes before help arrives, a calm person who knows the basic steps can prevent an injury from becoming worse.","Second, first aid training builds confidence and responsibility. Students who learn how to check whether someone is breathing, how to place a person in the recovery position and how to call emergency services clearly are more likely to act instead of freezing.","Some people argue that children are too young for such serious lessons and might panic or make mistakes. However, courses for young people focus on simple, safe actions, and organisations such as the Saudi Red Crescent already run training designed for students.","A few hours of training could one day save a life. That is a lesson no school can afford to skip."]},"items":[{"n":26,"t":"mc","s":"What is the author’s claim?","o":["Basic first aid should be a required part of the intermediate curriculum.","Students should be allowed to call emergency services themselves.","Teachers should always stay in the playground during break time to watch students.","Hospitals should open more emergency departments in every city."],"k":"A","sk":"Determines author’s main argument or claim","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":27,"t":"mc","s":"Which reason is given in the third paragraph?","o":["Emergencies happen at home and on trips as well as at school.","Training makes students more confident and likely to act.","Children are too young for serious lessons and may panic.","The Saudi Red Crescent runs training designed for students."],"k":"B","sk":"Identifies reasons that support a claim","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":28,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the author begin by asking the reader to imagine an emergency?","o":["to describe a real event at the author’s school","to show that teachers are often far away","to make the issue feel personal and urgent","to explain the steps of first aid in order"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes the purpose of an introduction","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":29,"t":"mc","s":"How does the author answer the counterclaim that children might panic?","o":["by saying that adults panic more often than children do","by explaining that student courses teach simple, safe actions","by ignoring it and moving straight to the conclusion","by agreeing that children should wait until they are adults"],"k":"B","sk":"Identifies a counterclaim and rebuttal","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":30,"t":"mc","s":"Which evidence would MOST strengthen the argument?","o":["a description of the uniforms worn by Red Crescent staff","a story about a student who enjoys science lessons","a list of all the subjects taught in intermediate schools across the country","data showing that trained bystanders improve survival in emergencies"],"k":"D","sk":"Evaluates evidence used to support claims","b":"221–230","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":null,"items":[{"n":31,"t":"mc","s":"The root **vis** means “see.” A **visible** star is one that —","o":["is very large","moves quickly","can be seen","has been named"],"k":"C","sk":"Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots in the 6-8 grade band","b":"191–200","g":"VO"},{"n":32,"t":"mc","s":"“The final exam is just around the corner.” What does this mean?","o":["The exam will happen very soon.","The exam is very difficult.","The exam is held in another room.","The exam has been cancelled."],"k":"A","sk":"Interprets idiom in context","b":"191–200","g":"VO"},{"n":33,"t":"mc","s":"The suffix **-less** means “without.” A **fearless** explorer is one who is —","o":["without fear","afraid again","full of fear","not exploring"],"k":"A","sk":"Understands how a suffix changes meaning","b":"191–200","g":"VO"},{"n":34,"t":"mc","s":"Read the sentence. “The two brothers had a **heated** argument, shouting so loudly that the neighbours heard them.” What does **heated** mean here?","o":["short and polite","angry and intense","quiet and secret","warm and pleasant"],"k":"B","sk":"Determines which meaning of a multiple-meaning word fits","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":35,"t":"mc","s":"Which word suggests the MOST extreme degree of tiredness?","o":["sleepy","tired","drowsy","exhausted"],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes nuances in meaning among related words","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":36,"t":"mc","s":"“After the long exam, my brain was mush.” This sentence is an example of —","o":["personification","a simile","a metaphor","onomatopoeia"],"k":"C","sk":"Interprets metaphor in context","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":37,"t":"mc","s":"Which word BEST completes the sentence? “Saad studied every evening; ___, his brother hardly opened a book.”","o":["for instance","in addition","as a result","in contrast"],"k":"D","sk":"Words that show a compare or contrast relationship","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":38,"t":"mc","s":"The root **tract** means “pull.” A **tractor** is a machine that —","o":["cuts grass","pulls heavy loads","measures land for farmers","sprays water"],"k":"B","sk":"Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots in the 6-8 grade band","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":39,"t":"mc","s":"Read the paragraph. “The old house was **dilapidated**. Its windows were broken, the roof had holes, and the front door hung from a single hinge.” What does **dilapidated** mean?","o":["in very bad condition","newly painted","very expensive and modern","full of people"],"k":"A","sk":"Uses paragraph-level context to determine word meaning","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":40,"t":"mc","s":"Which word has the most NEGATIVE connotation?","o":["bold","daring","reckless","brave"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes connotative meanings of words","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":41,"t":"mc","s":"Read the sentence. “The scientist’s **hypothesis** was that plants grow faster with more light, so she designed an experiment to test it.” What is a **hypothesis**?","o":["a piece of lab equipment","a type of plant food","a proven scientific law","an idea that can be tested"],"k":"D","sk":"Determines the meaning of academic words in context","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":42,"t":"mc","s":"Which word means almost the same as **abundant**?","o":["plentiful","empty","tiny","rare and hard to find"],"k":"A","sk":"Uses synonym relationships","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":43,"t":"mc","s":"“Cleaning the whole stadium in one day was a Herculean task.” Hercules was a hero of great strength. What does **Herculean** mean here?","o":["dangerous and forbidden","needing enormous strength or effort","done by one hero alone without help","very quick and easy"],"k":"B","sk":"Interprets allusion in context","b":"221–230","g":"VO"}]}]},{"id":"E","note":"Final full practice test · 43 questions · hardest","sections":[{"passage":{"title":"The Demonstration","genre":"drama","paras":["*(A school hall. RAKAN, 14, stands beside a laptop connected to a projector. Judges sit at a table. His older sister, LAMA, sits in the audience.)*","RAKAN: My app helps students find a study partner in their own neighbourhood. You just type your subjects and… *(He presses a key. The screen freezes.)* …and… one moment.","*(He presses again. Nothing happens. A judge looks at her watch.)*","RAKAN: *(quietly, to himself)* It worked this morning. It worked a hundred times.","LAMA: *(standing up, calmly)* Rakan, tell them why you built it.","RAKAN: *(after a pause, turning away from the screen)* Last year, I failed mathematics. I studied alone and I didn’t understand anything. Then I found a boy two streets away who was good at maths and bad at English. We helped each other, and we both passed. *(He looks at the judges.)* The app isn’t working right now. But the idea already worked. For me.","*(Silence. Then one judge begins to write.)*"]},"items":[{"n":1,"t":"mc","s":"What problem occurs during the demonstration?","o":["The projector shows the wrong student’s project.","The app freezes on the screen while the judges watch.","Rakan forgets the name of the app he has built.","Lama interrupts the judges while they are speaking."],"k":"B","sk":"Identifies conflict in literary text","b":"191–200","g":"LT"},{"n":2,"t":"mc","s":"What does Rakan’s line “It worked a hundred times” reveal?","o":["his pride in how many times he has shown the app to people","his plan to ask the judges for more time","his frustration and disbelief that it is failing now","his belief that the judges have broken it"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes dialogue to understand characters","b":"201–210","g":"LT"},{"n":3,"t":"mc","s":"Why is Lama’s suggestion important to the plot?","o":["It shifts Rakan’s focus from the broken app to the purpose behind it.","It shows the judges that Lama designed the app herself.","It ends the demonstration so that the next student can begin.","It allows Lama to fix the broken laptop quietly while Rakan is still talking."],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes how dialogue advances plot","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":4,"t":"mc","s":"What does the final stage direction suggest?","o":["The judges are writing down that the app has failed.","The audience has left the hall during the silence.","The judges are calling the next student to present.","The judges have been impressed by Rakan’s explanation."],"k":"D","sk":"Makes inferences from literary text","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":5,"t":"mc","s":"What does the stage direction *(turning away from the screen)* symbolise?","o":["Rakan moving beyond the technical failure to speak from experience","Rakan trying to hide his face from the audience","Rakan giving up on the competition completely and preparing to leave the hall","Rakan looking for his sister in the audience"],"k":"A","sk":"Interprets symbolism in literary text","b":"221–230","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"The Machine That Changed Reading","genre":"informational","paras":["Before the fifteenth century, most books in Europe were copied by hand. A single book could take months to produce, so books were rare and expensive, and few people owned one.","Around 1450, in the German city of Mainz, Johannes Gutenberg developed a printing press that used **movable type**: small metal letters that could be arranged into lines, inked, pressed onto paper and then rearranged for the next page. Printing with movable type was not entirely new; printers in China and Korea had used it centuries earlier. But Gutenberg’s system, with metal type, oil-based ink and a strong press, made it possible to print many copies quickly.","The results were dramatic. Within fifty years, printing presses were working in hundreds of European towns, and millions of books had been printed. As books became cheaper, more people learned to read. Scientists could share their findings more widely, and new ideas spread faster than ever before.","Not everyone welcomed the change. Some worried that printing would spread errors or dangerous ideas, and that copyists would lose their work. Yet the press could not be stopped, and many historians consider it one of the most important inventions in human history."]},"items":[{"n":6,"t":"mc","s":"What problem did the printing press help to solve?","o":["Copyists made too many mistakes in scientific books.","Hand-copied books were slow to produce, rare and expensive.","Books written in Chinese could not be read by most people in Europe.","Paper was too expensive for most printers to buy."],"k":"B","sk":"Identifies problem-solution as a structure","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":7,"t":"mc","s":"Which effect of printing is described in the third paragraph?","o":["Scientists stopped writing books and gave lectures.","Copyists found new jobs working in printing shops.","Governments banned books that contained errors.","More people learned to read as books became cheaper."],"k":"D","sk":"Determines the effect of an event","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":8,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the author mention printers in China and Korea?","o":["to show that Asian books were cheaper than European ones","to prove that Gutenberg copied his invention from Asia","to show that movable type existed before Gutenberg’s press","to explain why European books were written in Latin"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes how specific details contribute to meaning","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":9,"t":"mc","s":"How is the text mainly organized?","o":["as cause and effect, from the problem to the invention to its results","as a set of instructions for printing a book","as a biography that covers Gutenberg’s whole life","as a comparison of printing in Europe, China and Korea over many centuries"],"k":"A","sk":"Describes the organizational structure of a text","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":10,"t":"mc","s":"What is the purpose of the last paragraph?","o":["to argue that printing spread more errors than ideas","to show that the change was resisted but still proved enormously important","to describe the life of Johannes Gutenberg in Mainz","to explain how the printing press worked, step by step, from type to finished page"],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes how specific paragraphs contribute to meaning","b":"221–230","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"Should Video Games Count as a Sport?","genre":"viewpoints","paras":["**Viewpoint 1 — Majed, 15, competitive gamer:** Esports are a real sport. Top players train for hours every day, study strategy, and need lightning-fast reactions and teamwork. International tournaments fill stadiums and offer large prizes, and Riyadh has hosted one of the biggest esports events in the world. If chess can be called a sport, why not esports?","**Viewpoint 2 — Dr. Huda, sports doctor:** Esports demand skill, but sport should also mean physical exercise. Many young people already spend too long sitting in front of screens. When I see teenagers who cannot run a kilometre, I worry that calling gaming a sport sends the wrong message. Let us respect esports as a competition, but keep the word “sport” for activities that build the body."]},"items":[{"n":11,"t":"mc","s":"What is Majed’s main claim?","o":["Tournaments should offer smaller prizes.","Chess should not be called a sport.","Gamers should exercise more often.","Esports should be considered a real sport."],"k":"D","sk":"Determines author’s main argument or claim","b":"201–210","g":"IT"},{"n":12,"t":"mc","s":"On which point do Majed and Dr. Huda AGREE?","o":["Esports should not have tournaments.","Esports should be banned for teenagers.","Esports require real skill.","Esports are good physical exercise."],"k":"C","sk":"Compares and contrasts viewpoints in multiple texts","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":13,"t":"mc","s":"Which statement BEST describes Dr. Huda’s position?","o":["She wants esports players to become doctors.","She believes esports should be banned completely in all schools and clubs.","She respects esports as competitions but would not call them sports.","She thinks esports are better exercise than football."],"k":"C","sk":"Determines author’s viewpoint","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":14,"t":"mc","s":"What kind of evidence does Dr. Huda use that Majed does NOT?","o":["the size of the prizes offered at international tournaments","her professional experience with teenagers’ fitness","the example of chess as a sport","the training schedules of top players"],"k":"B","sk":"Evaluates evidence used to support claims","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":15,"t":"mc","s":"How does Majed use the example of chess?","o":["to show that chess players are much better and smarter than video gamers","to prove that esports are older than chess","to explain why stadiums are full for tournaments","to argue that a game without physical exercise can still be a sport"],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes reasoning in an argument","b":"221–230","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"The Oud Teacher","genre":"story","paras":["The first time Nasser heard Ustadh Kamal play the oud, he was ten years old and standing outside a shop in the old market. The music came through the half-open door like water through a crack in a wall, and he stood there until his mother called him three times.","Now, at thirteen, Nasser sat in that same shop every Tuesday afternoon. Ustadh Kamal was nearly blind, and he taught by listening. “Again,” he would say, before Nasser had even finished a phrase. “Your fingers are playing. Your ears are asleep.”","For months, Nasser hated those words. He practised until his fingertips were hard and sore, and still the old man said, “Again.” One evening, frustrated, Nasser played the piece as fast as he could and put the oud down with a bang. “There,” he said. “Every note was correct.”","Ustadh Kamal was silent for a long time. Then he picked up his own oud and played the same piece slowly, so slowly that Nasser could hear the space between each note, the way one note seemed to answer the one before it. When he finished, he said quietly, “Correct is where music begins. It is not where it ends.”","Nasser walked home without speaking. That night, he played the piece once more, very slowly, listening. And for the first time, he heard what had stopped him outside the shop three years before."]},"items":[{"n":16,"t":"mc","s":"How is the story structured in the first two paragraphs?","o":["It begins with a memory from three years earlier and then moves to the present.","It tells the same event twice from two different points of view.","It begins with the ending and then explains how it happened.","It begins in the present and then jumps forward many years to Nasser as an adult."],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes flashback as a structure","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":17,"t":"mc","s":"“The music came through the half-open door like water through a crack in a wall.” What does this simile suggest?","o":["The music sounded wet and unclear in the busy market.","The music was quiet and hard to hear because the shop door was nearly closed.","The music flowed out gently but powerfully and could not be held back.","The shop was old and its walls were badly damaged."],"k":"C","sk":"Interprets simile in context","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":18,"t":"mc","s":"What does Ustadh Kamal mean by “Your fingers are playing. Your ears are asleep”?","o":["Nasser’s fingers are too weak to press the strings.","Nasser plays the notes without truly listening to the music.","Nasser cannot hear well and needs to sit closer.","Nasser is far too tired to practise properly after a long day at school."],"k":"B","sk":"Determines the meaning of a nonliteral phrase","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":19,"t":"mc","s":"Why does the teacher respond to Nasser’s fast playing by playing slowly himself?","o":["to show Nasser what he is missing rather than simply telling him","to prove that he can still play much better than his young student can","to test whether Nasser remembers the order of the notes","to make Nasser wait before he is allowed to go home"],"k":"A","sk":"Explains character motivation","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":20,"t":"mc","s":"How does the final sentence connect to the opening of the story?","o":["Nasser realises that his mother was right to call him home.","Nasser forgets the piece that his teacher played that day.","Nasser decides to open his own music shop one day in the same old market.","Nasser finally hears the quality that first drew him to the music."],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes how an ending contributes to meaning","b":"221–230","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"The Well-Digger","genre":"poem","paras":["Each word I learn, I dig a little deeper,","Through sand and stone, through grammar, rule and sum;","Some days the ground is hard, the slope is steeper,","Some days I swear the water will not come.","","My teacher says: “The well is not for showing.","No traveller admires a hole in sand.","But when the summer comes and wells are going,","They’ll queue to drink from what you dug by hand.”","","So I keep digging, bucket after bucket,","And sometimes, in the dark, I hear it: there —","A cold, slow sound, as if the earth had shook it:","The first thin water, rising into air."],"poem":true},"items":[{"n":21,"t":"mc","s":"What is the extended metaphor in the poem?","o":["Water is compared to a difficult grammar rule.","Teaching is compared to travelling in a desert.","Summer is compared to a long examination.","Learning is compared to digging a well."],"k":"D","sk":"Interprets extended metaphor","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":22,"t":"mc","s":"What do the “hard” ground and “steeper” slope represent?","o":["the difficult days of study when progress is slow","the teacher’s strict rules in class","the high, rocky mountains near the speaker’s village","the heat of the summer months"],"k":"A","sk":"Interprets metaphor in context","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":23,"t":"mc","s":"What happens in the final stanza?","o":["The speaker gives up digging because the ground is too dry.","The speaker begins to see the first reward of his effort.","The speaker’s teacher finally fills the well with water.","The speaker hears travellers queuing to drink from the well."],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes how specific parts of a poem contribute to meaning","b":"211–220","g":"LT"},{"n":24,"t":"mc","s":"What does the teacher mean by “The well is not for showing”?","o":["Wells should be hidden from travellers who cross the desert at night.","Digging a well is more important than studying.","Learning is valuable for its use, not for impressing others.","Students should not show their work to the teacher."],"k":"C","sk":"Determines the meaning of a nonliteral phrase","b":"221–230","g":"LT"},{"n":25,"t":"mc","s":"What is the theme of the poem?","o":["Wells are the most important and valuable resource in the desert.","Teachers should make lessons easier for their students.","Patient, unseen effort eventually produces something valuable.","Travellers should dig their own wells instead of queuing."],"k":"C","sk":"Determines theme in poetry","b":"221–230","g":"LT"}]},{"passage":{"title":"When Arabia Was Green","genre":"informational","paras":["Today, the Nefud Desert in northern Saudi Arabia is a sea of red sand dunes. It is hard to imagine it as a land of lakes and grasslands. Yet scientists have found strong evidence that this is exactly what it was, many times over the last several hundred thousand years.","The evidence comes from the ground itself. In places, layers of white sediment lie beneath the dunes. These are the dried beds of ancient lakes. Researchers have also found fossils of animals that need plenty of water, including hippopotamuses, as well as stone tools made by early humans who lived beside the lakes.","Why did the climate change so dramatically? Scientists believe that small, regular changes in the Earth’s orbit shifted the paths of monsoon rains. During some periods, the rains moved far enough north to fill lakes and rivers across Arabia. During others, they retreated, and the desert returned.","These “Green Arabia” periods matter for understanding human history. Many researchers think that the wetter phases created routes along which early humans and animals could move between Africa and Asia. The red dunes, it turns out, are only the latest chapter in a much longer story."]},"items":[{"n":26,"t":"mc","s":"What is the central idea of the text?","o":["The Nefud Desert is the largest and most beautiful area of red sand dunes anywhere in the world today.","Evidence shows that Arabia was green many times in the past, which helps explain human history.","Hippopotamuses lived in northern Saudi Arabia until a few hundred years ago.","The monsoon rains always reach northern Arabia during the summer months."],"k":"B","sk":"Determines main/central idea in informational text","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":27,"t":"ms","s":"Which TWO kinds of evidence show that the Nefud was once wet? Choose TWO answers.","o":["ancient maps drawn by early travellers","dried lake beds beneath the dunes","satellite photographs of modern rainfall","records kept by weather stations","fossils of animals that need plenty of water"],"k":"B, E","sk":"Identifies evidence that supports a statement","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":28,"t":"mc","s":"According to the text, what caused the shifts between wet and dry periods?","o":["volcanoes that erupted many times and changed the shape of the desert landscape","early humans who cut down the forests beside the lakes","rising sea levels that flooded the land from the coast","changes in the Earth’s orbit that moved monsoon rains north and south"],"k":"D","sk":"Determines the cause of a situation","b":"211–220","g":"IT"},{"n":29,"t":"mc","s":"How does the author open the text?","o":["by contrasting the desert of today with a surprising picture of the past","by describing a scientist’s long and difficult journey into the Nefud Desert","by asking readers to protect the desert from damage","by listing the animals whose fossils have been discovered"],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes the purpose of an introduction","b":"221–230","g":"IT"},{"n":30,"t":"mc","s":"What does the final sentence suggest?","o":["The desert is only one stage in a long history of change.","Early humans preferred to live among red sand dunes.","Scientists have finished studying the history of Arabia.","The desert will become green again within a few years."],"k":"A","sk":"Analyzes how an ending contributes to meaning","b":"221–230","g":"IT"}]},{"passage":null,"items":[{"n":31,"t":"mc","s":"The prefix **trans-** means “across.” A **transatlantic** flight goes —","o":["around the Atlantic coast","across the Atlantic Ocean","under the Atlantic Ocean","away from the Atlantic Ocean"],"k":"B","sk":"Uses a defined root and/or affix to determine word meaning","b":"201–210","g":"VO"},{"n":32,"t":"mc","s":"The root **mort** means “death.” Which word describes something that will die one day?","o":["moral","immortal","mortar","mortal"],"k":"D","sk":"Applies knowledge of Greek or Latin roots in the 6-8 grade band","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":33,"t":"mc","s":"Read the paragraph. “The speaker was **eloquent**. She chose every word carefully, her sentences flowed smoothly, and the whole audience was moved.” What does **eloquent** mean?","o":["quiet and shy","loud and very angry","able to speak clearly and powerfully","nervous, shy and hard to understand"],"k":"C","sk":"Uses paragraph-level context to determine word meaning","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":34,"t":"mc","s":"In which sentence does **novel** mean “new and unusual”?","o":["The library has a shelf of novels for teenagers.","Her first novel was translated into ten languages.","The engineer suggested a novel way to cool the building.","He read a long novel about a sailor who was lost at sea."],"k":"C","sk":"Determines which meaning of a multiple-meaning word fits","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":35,"t":"mc","s":"“The silence was so thick you could cut it with a knife.” What does this suggest?","o":["Someone was cutting bread.","The silence felt very tense.","The room was very noisy.","The room was full of food."],"k":"B","sk":"Analyzes the effect of figurative language","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":36,"t":"mc","s":"Which word means the OPPOSITE of **diligent**?","o":["patient","busy","careful","lazy"],"k":"D","sk":"Identifies antonyms","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":37,"t":"mc","s":"Which analogy is CORRECT? **Author** is to **novel** as **architect** is to —","o":["building","garden","painting","song"],"k":"A","sk":"Uses analogies to understand word relationships","b":"211–220","g":"VO"},{"n":38,"t":"mc","s":"Which word suggests the MOST serious kind of mistake?","o":["typo","error","slip","blunder"],"k":"D","sk":"Analyzes nuances in meaning among related words","b":"221–230","g":"VO"},{"n":39,"t":"mc","s":"Which word BEST completes the sentence? “The evidence was weak; ___, the committee could not reach a decision.”","o":["accordingly","similarly","however","meanwhile"],"k":"A","sk":"Words that show a cause-effect relationship","b":"221–230","g":"VO"},{"n":40,"t":"mc","s":"Which word has the most NEGATIVE connotation?","o":["famous","celebrated","notorious","well-known"],"k":"C","sk":"Analyzes connotative meanings of words","b":"221–230","g":"VO"},{"n":41,"t":"mc","s":"“Opening that old box was like opening Pandora’s box.” In a Greek myth, Pandora opened a box that released troubles into the world. What does the comparison suggest?","o":["Opening the box made everyone laugh.","Opening the box caused many unexpected problems.","Opening the box revealed a great and valuable treasure.","Opening the box was very easy to do."],"k":"B","sk":"Interprets allusion in context","b":"221–230","g":"VO"},{"n":42,"t":"mc","s":"Read the sentence. “The judge listened to both sides so that she could remain **impartial**.” What does **impartial** mean?","o":["tired of the argument","fair and not taking sides","certain of the answer","angry and impatient with both sides"],"k":"B","sk":"Uses context to determine word meaning","b":"221–230","g":"VO"},{"n":43,"t":"mc","s":"“A **deafening silence** filled the room.” This phrase combines two opposite ideas. What is this called, and what does it suggest?","o":["a simile; the silence sounded just like very loud music in the room","hyperbole; everyone in the room became deaf","personification; the silence spoke to the people","an oxymoron; the silence felt very strong and uncomfortable"],"k":"D","sk":"Interprets oxymoron in context","b":"221–230","g":"VO"}]}]}],"pass":[{"title":"The New Boy","genre":"story","paras":["When Mr. Saleh moved the new student, Yusuf, into the seat beside Hassan, Hassan sighed loudly. He had saved that seat for his best friend all year. Yusuf had arrived from Sudan two weeks earlier. He spoke Arabic with an accent the class found funny, and he hardly ever smiled.","“He thinks he is better than us,” Hassan told his friends at break. “He never laughs at our jokes.”","For days, Hassan pretended not to notice when Yusuf dropped his pencil or searched for the right page. Then, one afternoon, the class was given a group project on rivers of Africa. Hassan was paired with Yusuf. He was reluctant, but he had no choice.","Yusuf knew everything about the Nile. He described how the Blue Nile and the White Nile meet at Khartoum, and he drew the meeting point from memory. As he drew, his face changed. “My father used to take me there on Fridays,” he said quietly. “He is still in Khartoum, in the hospital. We could not bring him.”","Hassan put down his pen. He thought about all the jokes, and about how he had assumed that silence meant pride. The next morning he arrived early and left a small gesture on Yusuf’s desk: a hand-drawn map of the school, with the canteen, the library and Hassan’s own house marked in red. At the bottom he had written, “In case you get lost. I live near here too.”","Yusuf read it twice. Then, for the first time, he smiled."]},{"title":"Saad’s Silence","genre":"story","paras":["Every morning at break, the same group of boys gathered by the water fountain, and every morning they found someone to laugh at. This week it was Majed, whose glasses were held together with tape.","“Professor Tape!” Ali called, and the others laughed. Saad laughed too, though not very loudly. He told himself it was only a joke. Majed pushed his glasses up and walked away without a word.","On Thursday, Saad saw Majed sitting alone behind the library, carefully winding new tape around the broken frame. His hands were shaking. Saad wanted to say something, but he did not know what, so he turned around and left.","That night, Saad could not sleep. He kept seeing Majed’s shaking hands. He realised that he had not said a single cruel word, yet his laughter had been part of the cruelty.","On Sunday, when Ali shouted, “Here comes Professor Tape!”, Saad did not laugh. He felt his face grow hot. “Leave him alone,” he said. His voice was quieter than he wanted, but the boys heard it. Ali stared at him, shrugged and turned away.","Later, Saad found Majed in the library. “I’m sorry,” he said. “I laughed when I shouldn’t have.” Majed looked at him for a long moment. Then he moved his bag off the chair beside him."]},{"title":"How Coffee Spread from Yemen","genre":"informational","paras":["Today, coffee is one of the most popular drinks in the world, and in Saudi Arabia a small cup of *qahwa* is a sign of welcome. Yet coffee drinking has a surprisingly short history compared with tea, and its story begins in the mountains of Yemen.","**From Bean to Drink**","The coffee plant probably grew wild in Ethiopia, across the Red Sea. By the fifteenth century, farmers in Yemen had begun to cultivate it on terraces cut into steep mountainsides. Scholars and worshippers there roasted the beans and boiled them, discovering that the drink helped them stay awake for long nights of study and prayer.","**Across the Region**","Merchants carried coffee north along the trade routes. By the early sixteenth century, it was being drunk in Makkah and Cairo, and by the middle of that century coffee houses had opened in Istanbul. These coffee houses became places to meet, talk and hear the news. Some rulers worried that people gathered there to criticise them, and coffee was even banned for short periods, but the bans never lasted.","**To the World**","For many years, the Yemeni port of Mocha was the centre of the world coffee trade. Later, traders took plants to other parts of the world, where huge plantations were created. Today most coffee comes from countries far from Arabia, but the region’s traditions of preparing and serving coffee remain an important part of daily life and hospitality."]},{"title":"The Great Green Wall","genre":"informational","paras":["South of the Sahara Desert lies the Sahel, a long belt of dry grassland that stretches across Africa from the Atlantic Ocean to the Red Sea. For decades, droughts, overgrazing and the cutting of trees have damaged the land there, and in many places the desert has advanced.","In 2007, a group of African countries launched an ambitious plan called the **Great Green Wall**. The original idea was to plant a line of trees about 8,000 kilometres long across the continent, like a living barrier against the desert.","Over time, the plan changed. Experts pointed out that a single line of trees would be hard to water and protect. Instead, the project now aims to restore a mosaic of farms, grasslands and woodlands. Local farmers are encouraged to protect young trees that grow naturally from old roots, to build small stone walls that slow rainwater, and to plant crops that hold the soil.","Progress has been slower than hoped. Funding has arrived late, and conflict in some areas has made work difficult. Still, millions of hectares have been restored, and in some villages farmers report better harvests and more water in their wells. Supporters say the project shows that fighting desertification depends as much on people as on trees."]},{"title":"Roots, Clues and Shades of Meaning","genre":"sentences","paras":["Many English words are built from Greek and Latin parts. If you know that **spect** means *look*, you can work out that a **spectator** is someone who watches.","**Context clues** are words nearby that explain a new word. Look for a definition, an example, a contrast (*but, however, unlike*) or a cause (*because, so*).","Words with similar meanings can have different **connotations**, or feelings. *Curious* sounds positive; *nosy* sounds negative.","**Figurative language** says more than the literal words: “The classroom was a furnace” means the room was very hot."]},{"title":"The Last Ferry","genre":"story","paras":["I almost missed the last ferry to the Farasan Islands. By the time my uncle and I ran up the ramp, the sun was already sliding toward the horizon, turning the water the colour of copper.","At first the journey was beautiful. Flying fish skimmed beside the boat like silver stones, and the passengers laughed and pointed. But halfway across, the wind changed. The copper sea turned grey, and a long, slow swell began to lift the ferry and drop it again, lift it and drop it.","The laughing stopped. A baby cried somewhere below deck. I gripped the cold rail and watched the lights of Jizan disappear behind us, while ahead there was nothing but gloom. My uncle, who had made this crossing a hundred times, kept talking calmly about the gazelles we would see on the islands, but I noticed that he was holding the rail too.","Then, through the darkness, a single light appeared, then another, then a whole curved line of them. “Farasan,” my uncle said, and his voice sounded lighter. The sea seemed to calm almost at once, though perhaps it was only me.","That night, lying in my bed in my aunt’s house, I could still feel the floor rising and falling. But I also remembered the moment the lights appeared, and I decided that it was the most vivid thing I had ever seen."]},{"title":"The Old Port","genre":"poem","paras":["The cranes are tall and silent now,","Their long necks bent above the quay;","Where once a hundred dhows would bow","And sail for India and the sea.","","My grandfather can name each one:","The *Pearl*, the *Swift*, the *Morning Star*;","He says their sails could hide the sun,","He says their songs were heard from far.","","Now steel ships pass, too big to land,","And no one sings, and no one waits;","But when he takes me by the hand,","The old port opens all its gates."],"poem":true},{"title":"Every School Should Teach Cooking","genre":"argument","paras":["Most students can use a smartphone in seconds, yet many cannot cook a simple, nutritious meal. Every intermediate school should offer a weekly cooking class.","First, cooking is a life skill. Young people who can prepare their own food are more independent and less likely to depend on fast food, which is often high in salt, sugar and fat.","Second, cooking teaches other subjects in a practical way. Measuring ingredients uses fractions, following a recipe develops reading skills, and understanding why bread rises is a science lesson.","Some people argue that schools are already too busy and that cooking should be taught at home. It is true that the timetable is full. However, many families have little time to teach cooking, and one period a week could replace part of a lesson that already covers the same skills, such as practical mathematics.","A student who can cook a healthy meal is ready for life. Isn’t that what school is for?"]},{"title":"Our City Needs Safe Bicycle Lanes","genre":"argument","paras":["On cool winter evenings, the streets of our neighbourhood fill with young people on bicycles. But they ride between parked cars and fast traffic, and many parents will not let their children ride at all. The city should build protected bicycle lanes in every neighbourhood.","First, safe lanes would make cycling a real way to travel. A trip of three kilometres to school or the shops takes only about fifteen minutes by bicycle. If students could ride safely, fewer parents would need to drive them, and morning traffic would be lighter.","Second, cycling is good for health. Regular exercise strengthens the heart and helps people manage stress. A lane that makes exercise part of the daily journey could help many families.","Some people say that our summer is far too hot for cycling. This is a fair point: few people will ride at 45 °C. However, the weather is pleasant for many months of the year, and trees planted along the lanes would give shade and make the city greener at the same time.","Finally, cycling is the future, and everybody already agrees that cars are a terrible way to travel."]},{"title":"Text 1: The Morning of the Eagles · Text 2: Birds on the Move","genre":"paired","paras":["**Text 1: The Morning of the Eagles (a narrative)**","My cousin Badr woke me before dawn. “Hurry,” he whispered, “or we will miss them.” We drove to a hill outside the town and waited with his binoculars. For an hour, nothing happened. Then, as the sun warmed the rocks, the sky began to fill. First one eagle, then ten, then more than I could count, circling higher and higher without moving their wings. “They are riding the warm air,” Badr said. “Tomorrow they will be in Africa.” I watched until my neck hurt, and I felt very small and very lucky.","**Text 2: Birds on the Move (an article)**","Every autumn, millions of birds fly south from Europe and Asia to spend the winter in Africa, and every spring they return. Many of them cross the Arabian Peninsula, which lies on one of the world’s great flyways. Large birds such as eagles and storks save energy by soaring on thermals, columns of warm air that rise as the sun heats the ground. Because thermals form only after the land warms up, these birds usually begin flying in the late morning. Scientists count migrating birds to check the health of each species, and many populations of large birds of prey have fallen in recent decades."]},{"title":"Text 1: The Day the Lights Came On · Text 2: Electricity Reaches the Villages","genre":"paired","paras":["**Text 1: The Day the Lights Came On (a memoir told by a grandfather)**","I was nine years old when the electricity reached our village in the mountains. For weeks, men had been raising tall poles along the road. On the evening it was switched on, the whole village gathered in the square. Someone pressed a switch, and a single bulb above the mosque door glowed white. Nobody spoke. An old man near me covered his eyes with his hand, and I thought he was afraid. Later I understood he was crying. He had spent his whole life reading by the light of a kerosene lamp.","**Text 2: Electricity Reaches the Villages (an article)**","In many countries, electricity reached large cities long before it reached rural villages. Building power lines across mountains and deserts is expensive, and each village has few customers. Governments therefore often paid for rural electrification as a public service. The effects were wide-ranging: pumps could lift water from deep wells, refrigerators could keep food and medicine cold, and children could study after dark. In Saudi Arabia, a major expansion of the national electricity network from the 1970s onward brought power to thousands of towns and villages."]}]};
(function(d){d.units.forEach(function(u){u.sets.forEach(function(s){s.passage=s.p==null?null:d.pass[s.p];});});})(window.MAP_DATA["reading_7"]);
